    <section xml:id="sec-accel-day13">
        <title>Day 13 In-Class: The Accelerometer as an I2C Device</title>

        <introduction>
            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-30, from the slides Petra passed. -->
            <p>
                On Day 10 we wrote a device driver for the seven-segment
                display, on the I2C bus.  You read about the LSM303AGR
                accelerometer, its breakout board, and its WHO_AM_I register.
                Today we'll wire the accelerometer onto the bus, verify that
                we can talk to it, and watch the transactions themselves on
                the logic analyzer.  We'll also make a start on its device
                driver.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-back-to-i2c">
            <title>Part 1: Back to I2C, a Second Device on the Bus</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-30.  The reveal paragraph below the
                 activity is load-bearing (Lab 7 D2 depends on it). -->
            <p>
                The LSM303AGR is an I2C device: it joins the same two-wire
                bus we have been using, speaks the same protocol, and is
                driven through the same I2C library: the five functions we
                have been calling since the display are all we need to talk
                to it.  It also has a magnetometer, but we'll only use the
                accelerometer in this class.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-back-to-i2c">
    <ul>
        <li>The LSM303AGR is an I2C device: it joins the same two-wire bus we have been using, with SDA and SCL held HIGH by pull-up resistors and every device tapped onto the same two wires.</li>
        <li>It speaks the same protocol and uses the same I2C library: the five functions we have been calling since the display are all we need to talk to it.</li>
        <li>It also has a magnetometer, but we'll only use the accelerometer in this class.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min. The sixty-second resurface: same bus, same protocol, same library — then the device in one look. The bus picture is deliberately NOT on this slide (it is the picture-form answer to the commit that follows); it projects on the reveal instead. Do NOT answer the display question here.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-keep-display">
                <title>Do I have to take the display down?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Suppose your seven-segment display is still connected
                    to the I2C pins of your Nucleo, and today we are adding the
                    accelerometer, a second I2C device.  Do you
                    need to take the display down first?  What if you wanted to
                    use both the accelerometer and the display in one project?
                    Commit to an answer, and to a reason, before we discuss
                    it.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-keep-display">
                <p>No, the display stays.  Both devices share the same two
                wires, and each has its own address: every transaction opens
                with the address of the device the controller wants to
                communicate with, so the
                display simply does not respond to transactions addressed to
                the accelerometer, and the other way around.  Letting several
                devices share two wires is the reason the bus exists.  In
                Lab 7 you will run both devices on one bus, so leave the
                display wired.</p>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_bus_two_wires.svg" width="68%"/>
            </instructor>

            <!-- The instructor block above and the student paragraph below
                 are two tellings of ONE answer (P-10 warns the pair drifts
                 invisibly): change them together.  The bus picture projects
                 on the reveal, not the resurface slide — P-15, Gate 2'
                 item 10. -->
            <p>
                No, the display stays: both devices share the same two
                wires, and each has its own address.  Every transaction opens
                with the address of the device the controller wants to
                communicate with, so the
                display simply does not respond to transactions addressed to
                the accelerometer, and the other way around.  Letting several
                devices share two wires is the reason the bus exists, and in
                Lab 7 you will run both devices on one bus, so leave the
                display wired.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-wire-and-prove">
            <title>Part 2: Wire It and Verify the Link</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-30.  fig-accel-wiring is her slide 8
                 rebuilt with her annotated cable photo (stemma.png,
                 2026-08-30); fig-accel-bb is her own whole-bus Fritzing. -->
            <p>
                We'll wire the sensor to the bus with four leads, and then
                run a short test program to check that the STM32C031C6 can
                talk to it.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-wiring">
                <caption>The LSM303AGR breakout and its STEMMA QT cable.  Either
                socket on the breakout takes the cable's plug, and it only fits
                one way.  Its four wires go to the Nucleo: black to GND,
                red to 3.3 V, yellow to SCL (D15, PB8), and blue to SDA
