            <activity xml:id="act-day13-whoami">
                <title>Wire the accelerometer and verify the link</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>The program we'll run, <c>whoami_test.c</c>, reads a
                    certain register, called WHO_AM_I, once a second and
                    prints whether it got the value it expected.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-wire">
                    <statement><p>Plug the STEMMA QT cable into the breakout
                    and wire its four leads against
                    <xref ref="fig-accel-wiring"/>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-project">
                    <statement><p>Make a copy of your <c>TemplateProject</c>
                    and call the new project <c>Accelerometer</c>.  Download
                    <c>whoami_test.c</c> from Canvas and put it in the
                    project's <c>Src</c> folder.  Build and upload.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-coolterm">
                    <statement><p>Open CoolTerm.  A working setup prints
                    <c>Accelerometer initialized!</c> once a second.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-what-ran">
            <title>Part 3: What Just Ran</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-30. -->
            <p>
                Here is the whole of <c>whoami_test.c</c>.  Notice the first
                <c>#define</c>: the device address is written
                <c>(0x32 &gt;&gt; 1)</c>, a number shifted right by one
                rather than written out.  <c>&gt;&gt;</c> moves every bit of a
                value one place toward the least significant end, dropping the
                bit that falls off the bottom and bringing in a 0 at the top;
                it is the mirror of the <c>&lt;&lt;</c> we have used on
                register bits since the first GPIO write.  What that does to
                <c>0x32</c>, we'll come back to, and to why the address
                is written this way, once we have the logic analyzer on the
                bus.
            </p>

            <!-- The listing below is her whoami_test.c verbatim
                 (check_starters.py enforces the code).  Her file's stale
                 "Table 13" citation was corrected to Table 22 in the
                 starter itself with her approval (2026-08-30), so book and
                 file agree; ground truth §2/§3 has the history. -->
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#include "i2c.h"

#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL       (0x32 >> 1)  // 7-bit address 0b0011001
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A		   0x0F	  		// default value: 00110011

uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

int main(void) {
	uint8_t who_am_i = 0;

	i2c1_init();
	uart2_init();
	printf("LSM303AGR initialization test \n\r");

	while(1) {
		who_am_i = lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A);
		if( who_am_i == 0x33 ) {
			printf("Accelerometer initialized! \n\r");
		}
		else {
			printf("Could not connect to accelerometer \n\r");
		}
		delay_ms(1000);
	}
	return 0;
}

/* Read one byte from an accelerometer register (Datasheet, Table 22) */
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

 	return data;
}
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The program has the same shape as the programs we have been
                writing all term: initialize the two peripherals, print a
                startup message, and then a loop that does one thing once a
                second: read WHO_AM_I, compare what came back to the number
                0x33, and
                print one of two lines.  The only function it
                defines besides <c>main()</c>,
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c>, is a single call into the
                I2C library from <xref ref="ch-i2c"/>:
                <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>, asked for one byte from one register.
                Receiving 0x33 back from the accelerometer as the contents of
                the WHO_AM_I register verifies the whole read path: the
                wiring, the device address, the protocol and the code all had
                to work for that value to arrive.  If it prints
                <c>Could not connect to accelerometer</c> instead, that is
                data too.  The analyzer we are about to put on the bus shows
                exactly what the wire is doing.
            </p>

<!-- The two WALL listings below deliberately drop the file's
     "// 7-bit address 0b0011001" comment — it is the answer to Part 5's
     why-19 puzzle (P-15).  It stays in the verbatim Part 3 listing above
     (byte-identity with her file).  Restore neither. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-whoami-main">
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL       (0x32 >> 1)
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A		   0x0F	  		// default value: 0x33 (00110011)
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

int main(void) {
	uint8_t who_am_i = 0;
	i2c1_init();
	uart2_init();
	printf("LSM303AGR initialization test \n\r");
	while(1) {
		who_am_i = lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A);
		if( who_am_i == 0x33 ) {
			printf("Accelerometer initialized! \n\r");
		}
		else {
			printf("Could not connect to accelerometer \n\r");
		}
		delay_ms(1000);
	}
	return 0;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Walk it top down: the loop reads WHO_AM_I once a second, forever. Say out loud that the address is written (0x32 &gt;&gt; 1) — and that we are not explaining the shift yet; the analyzer will. The listing is abridged for the wall (#includes, the address comment and the blank lines are off it); the full file is in the book and in Src.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-whoami-read">
    <p>Recall that the I2C library contains five functions: <c>i2c1_init()</c>, <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c>, <c>i2c1_byteRead()</c>, <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> and <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>.  To read the contents of a register from the accelerometer we use <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>, via the following function:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* Read one byte from an accelerometer register (Datasheet, Table 22) */
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

 	return data;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Five functions, named once on the wall — this is the one that does register reads.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-whoami-proves">
    <ul>
        <li>Receiving 0x33 back from the accelerometer as the contents of the WHO_AM_I register verifies the whole read path: the wiring, the device address, the protocol, and the code all had to work for that value to arrive.</li>
        <li>It is not yet evidence about how the sensor is configured, since the control registers are still at their power-on defaults, and setting them up is work still ahead of us.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. This is the reading's WHO_AM_I point, said once in class: proof of communication, not of configuration.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-analyzer">
            <title>Part 4: Put the Analyzer on It</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-30.
                 P-6: act-day13-predict-decoder and act-day13-capture come
                 BEFORE fig-whoami-capture, which answers them.  Do not
                 reorder. -->

            <p>
                The line CoolTerm prints tells us that the read worked, but
                it does not show us what happened on the two wires.  To see
                the transactions themselves (the address going out, the
                register number, and the byte coming back), we'll put the
                AD2's logic analyzer on the bus, and we'll work from that
                trace for the rest of class.
            </p>

            <p>
                We'll put the AD2's digital leads on the two wires with the
                same setup we used on Day 9x
                (<xref ref="fig-ad2-digital-wiring"/>), with one change: this
                read is two transactions and takes about 400 µs, so in
                Waveforms we'll set the time base to 50 µs per division
                rather than 20, and drag the view so that the first START is
                at the left edge.
            </p>

            <!-- Gate 2' item 5: the synthesizer's preferred asset (derive
                 leads from Day 10's display_wiring_ad2.svg) turned out
                 infeasible — that SVG is a bitmap photo of the SCOPE-lead
                 setup, not editable vector leads.  Its sanctioned fallback
                 ships instead: her slide 14's clean AD2 product photo
                 (which-bundle), pin mapping in the caption; the real
                 loaded-breadboard setup photo is on the ask-Petra list. -->
<!-- The connector-labels diagram is the pinout sheet from her slide 25
     (its printed title, cropped off here, says ADALM2000 — the lead
     numbering and colors match the AD2 harness she teaches from, and it
     is her own long-standing slide asset).  Flagged in the delivery
     report in case she has a true AD2 pinout to swap in. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-ad2-digital-wiring">
                <caption>The AD2's digital channels are the larger flying-lead
                bundle (left, plugged into the AD2), and the pinout sheet
                (right) says which lead is which: the digital leads are
                numbered 0–15, and the ground leads carry the ⏚ symbol.
                Three leads go onto the bus: DIO0 (pink, lead 0 on the
                sheet) to the SDA row, DIO1 (green, lead 1) to the SCL row,
                and a ground lead (black) to ground.  The
                STEMMA leads and the display stay where they are.  (The sheet
                is drawn for the AD2's sibling instrument; the digital
                numbering, colors and grounds are identical, only the trigger
                pins are named differently.)</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="34% 62%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
                    <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/ad2_digital_bundle.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/ad2_connector_labels_hires.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-analyzer-wiring" ref="fig-ad2-digital-wiring">
    <p>The AD2's digital channels are the larger flying-lead bundle.  Three leads go onto the bus:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>A ground lead (black) to ground.</li>
        <li>DIO0 (the first pink lead) to the SDA row.</li>
        <li>DIO1 (the first green) to the SCL row.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>The digital leads are numbered 0–15 on the sheet (lead 0 is DIO0, lead 1 is DIO1), and the ground leads carry the ⏚ symbol.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min. The wiring is the recall from Day 9x; early finishers did it during the build. The pinout strip is how to find lead 0, lead 1 and a ground lead in the bundle. The STEMMA leads and the display stay where they are.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-analyzer-waveforms" ref="fig-waveforms-decode">
    <ul>
        <li>The setup is Day 9x's, unchanged: DIO1 as clock, DIO0 as data, DIO0 falling as the trigger, single sweep.</li>
        <li>One change: 50 µs/div.  This read is two transactions and spans about 400 µs; a 20 µs/div window is 200 µs wide and cannot hold it.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. The room did this on Day 9x; do not re-teach the decoder steps. The one thing that is new is the time base — the screenshot is Day 9x's and its axis still shows 20 µs/div; the second bullet carries the change.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-predict-decoder">
                <title>Predict what the decoder will show</title>
                <statement>
                    <p><c>whoami_test.c</c> is running: once a second, the
                    loop calls <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c>, which is one
                    call to <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>.  Before you capture
                    anything, predict what the protocol decoder will show for
                    one pass through the loop.  How many transactions?  What
                    goes out in each one, and what comes back?  Write your
                    prediction down.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-predict-code" room="yes">
    <p>Predict, before you capture: when this loop runs, what will the protocol decoder show?  How many transactions, and what bytes in each one?</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL  (0x32 >> 1)
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A     0x0F          // default value: 00110011

// called from the loop in main(), once a second:
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

 	return data;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 4 min, committed in writing. The listing is abridged to the lines the prediction needs (the address comment is deliberately off the wall — P-15); the full file was just walked and is in the book. Collect two or three predictions out loud without ruling on them — most get the pieces right and the number of transactions wrong, and the trace settles it, not you.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-capture">
                <title>Capture the WHO_AM_I read</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-sweep">
                    <statement><p>Put the AD2's digital leads on the bus as
                    above, set up Waveforms and the decoder as on Day 9x
                    (with the time base at 50 µs per division), and take a
                    single sweep.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-read-decode">
                    <statement><p>Read your decoded trace and find three
                    things: the device address the decoder prints, the
                    register address, and the value the accelerometer sent
                    back.  Compare them with the two <c>#define</c>s at the
                    top of <c>whoami_test.c</c>, open in your own IDE, and
                    with your prediction.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture">
                <caption>The WHO_AM_I read, captured and decoded.  Two
                transactions cross the screen: <c>h19 WR</c>, its ACK, the
                register address <c>h0F</c>, its ACK, and a STOP, then
                <c>h19 RD</c>, its ACK, the returned <c>h33</c>, a NACK, and
                the final STOP.  SCL is DIO1 (green) and SDA is DIO0 (pink),
                as wired on Day 9x.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_c.png" width="97%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-capture-c" ref="fig-whoami-capture">
    <caption>Two transactions: h19 WR · A · h0F · A · P, then h19 RD · A · h33 · N · P, where A is an acknowledge, N a NACK and P a STOP.  Find the device address, the register address and the returned value on your own screen.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min, and the Part's rescue: a student who cannot get a sweep marks this projected capture instead — the decoded values are the same by construction. The next slide has the same capture close up, large enough to read the bytes from the back. Checkpoint at minute 65 is the end of this Part.</note>
</slide>

            <!-- Gate 2' item 19 fallback, shipped 2026-08-27: Petra exports
                 one way (her words), so the B/C re-export ask is closed and
                 the sanctioned split ships — the whole strip stays as the
                 discovery and the rescue's marking target; the close-up
                 halves are where the bytes are read.  Do not merge them
                 back. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture-closeup">
                <caption>The same capture, close up: the write transaction
                (top: the device address and the register address going
                out), then the read transaction (bottom: the device address
                again, and the 0x33 coming back, ended by the controller's
                NACK).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_c_write.png" width="97%"/>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_c_read.png" width="76%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-capture-c-closeup" ref="fig-whoami-capture-closeup">
    <caption>Between the halves: a STOP, then a fresh START.  The same h19 goes out again, this time RD.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The code walk's companion is the transfer diagram one slide back, not this one — no backward jump needed.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-predict-decoder">
                <p>Two transactions, one right after the other.  The decoder
                reads: <c>h19 WR</c>, ACK, <c>h0F</c>, ACK, STOP (the device
                address and the register address going out), then
                <c>h19 RD</c>, ACK, <c>h33</c>, NACK, STOP (the device
                address again, and the register's contents coming back).  Most
                predictions get the pieces right and the <em>two
                transactions</em> wrong: our <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> ends the
                first transaction and starts a second one.  That, the 0x19,
                and the final NACK are what Part 5 digs into.  Let the room
                find them on their own traces first.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-digging-deeper">
            <title>Part 5: Digging Deeper</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-30. -->
            <p>
                You now have a decoded trace on your screen.  We'll work
                through the questions it raises, and we can answer every one
                of them from the trace itself.
            </p>

<!-- Gate 2' item 13: why-19 is its own activity, answered aloud on the
     spot, BEFORE the two committed questions — the plan's staging, now in
     the source rather than only in a presenter note. -->
            <activity xml:id="act-day13-why-19">
                <title>Why 0x19?</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-why-19">
                    <statement><p>The program writes the device address as
                    <c>(0x32 &gt;&gt; 1)</c>.  Where does the 0x32 come from,
                    and why does the decoder print <c>h19</c>?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-digging-deeper">
                <title>Digging deeper, against your own trace</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Answer these from your own decoded trace.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-address-twice">
                    <statement><p>On Day 10 we saw that our
                    <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> sends the register address, lets
                    that transaction end, and starts a second one.  Confirm it
                    on the trace in front of you: find where the first
                    transaction ends and the second begins, and what the
                    device address is doing there a second time.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-who-acks">
                    <statement><p>Find every acknowledge bit on the trace in
                    front of you.  There is one after each byte.  For each
                    one: who sent it, and is it an ACK or a NACK?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-digging-deeper">
                <p>0x32 &gt;&gt; 1 = 0b00110010 &gt;&gt; 1 = 0b0011001 = 0x19.
                The datasheet gives the 8-bit form with the R/W bit attached
                (Table 24, p. 39); the decoder prints the 7-bit address, which
                is what actually identifies the device: Day 9x's shift,
                paying out.  The 0x7F test from Day 9x cannot settle this
                one, since 0x32 is below 0x7F either way; the datasheet's own
                labelling is what does.</p>
                <p>The address goes out twice because the read is two
                transactions: a write that delivers the register address, a
                STOP, then a fresh START and the same address with the read
                bit.</p>
                <p>The acknowledge bits after both <c>h19</c>s and after
                <c>h0F</c> are the accelerometer's.  It acknowledges its own
                address once in each transaction.  The bit after <c>h33</c>
                is a NACK, and it is the <em>controller's</em>, sent on
                purpose: one byte was asked for, one byte arrived, and the
                NACK says <q>no more</q>.</p>
            </instructor>

            <p>
                Two different things on this bus are both called a NACK, a
                not-acknowledge.  The NACK on your trace is the
                <em>controller's</em>, and it is part of a successful read:
                the controller asked for one byte, got it, and deliberately
                does not acknowledge, which tells the target to stop sending.
                The other kind is the one we saw on Day 9x and produced on
                Day 10: nothing pulls SDA LOW during the acknowledge bit, and
                the wire simply stays HIGH.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-two-nacks">
    <ul>
        <li>The NACK on your trace is the <em>controller's</em>, and it is part of a successful read: one byte was asked for, one byte arrived, and by not acknowledging, the controller ends the read.</li>
        <li>The other kind of NACK comes from nobody at all: if no device recognizes the address, nothing pulls SDA LOW during the acknowledge bit, and the wire simply stays HIGH.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Name both kinds now — the second one is about to be produced on purpose.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-transfer-pattern">
    <p>The datasheet's own drawing of what you captured, in four tables: its <em>Master</em> row is our controller, its <em>Slave</em> row our target.  The write transfers first:</p>
    <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/i2c_transfer_writes.svg" width="92%"/>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Say the vocabulary once, off the lead line. Two Digging Deeper answers live here: the controller sends the address, and the register address goes out as SUB.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-transfer-reads">
    <p>Table 22 is the shape your trace has, and NMAK is the N on your trace.</p>
    <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/i2c_transfer_reads.svg" width="92%"/>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The last Digging Deeper answer: the data comes back from the target in the read half. Where the table draws SR, a repeated START, our trace shows a STOP and a fresh START — the close-up slide already showed it. If anyone asks whether ours is wrong: Day 10's answer. A STOP frees the bus, and with a second controller on it the register pointer you just set could be changed before you read. One controller here, so it is safe.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The transfer diagram in <xref ref="fig-i2c-transfer-pattern"/>
                is the datasheet's own drawing of what you captured: §6.1.1,
                Tables 20–23, and Table 22 is the shape your trace has.  Three
                more questions can be answered from its rows: who sends the
                address, where we tell the accelerometer which register to
                read from, and where the accelerometer puts the register's
                contents on the data line.  Where our trace shows a STOP and a
                fresh START between the two halves of the read, the datasheet
                draws a repeated START; the register-level walk of
                <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> in <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-library"/>
                shows exactly where our library makes that choice, and why it
                matters on a bus with two controllers.  The NACK you found on
                your own trace is in neither half of that listing: the library
                describes the whole transaction in advance, how many bytes
                (NBYTES) and what to do at the end (AUTOEND).  The I2C
                hardware puts the acknowledge bits and the STOP on the wire.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-memread-write">
    <p>Everything you captured came from one function, <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>.  Here is the half of it that produces your trace's <em>first</em> transaction:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void i2c1_memRead(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t registerAddr, uint8_t nbytes, uint8_t *data) {
	// Ping target address for writing 1 byte (the register address)
	I2C1->CR2 = (deviceAddr<<1)                  // target address
			    | (1U << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos)     // for registerAddr
				| I2C_CR2_AUTOEND                // Send Stop condition after transfer
			    | I2C_CR2_START;				 // Send Start condition
	while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_BUSY) );		 // Wait until Start is received

	if( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_NACKF) ) {		  // Make sure ACK received
		// Send register address
		while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_TXIS) );     // Wait for Tx to be ready
		I2C1->TXDR = registerAddr;			      // Send register address
	}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 3 min, the first in-class walk of this code (the fact it explains — stop-then-start — is Day 10's). One write to CR2 describes the whole transaction before any of it happens: who, how many bytes, AUTOEND, START — Day 10's byteWrite pattern. Trailing comments are shortened for the wall; the full commentary is in i2c.c and the ch-i2c Reference.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-memread-read">
    <p>Then the same function starts over (a second CR2 write, this time with the read bit), and that is your trace's <em>second</em> transaction:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	// Restart, with read request for n bytes
	I2C1->CR2 = (deviceAddr<<1)                  // target address
			    | (nbytes << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos) // will read n bytes
				| I2C_CR2_RD_WRN                 // ping for reading
			    | I2C_CR2_AUTOEND                // Send Stop condition after transfer
				| I2C_CR2_START;				 // Send Start condition
	while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_BUSY) );		 // Wait until Start is received

	// Read the data
	if( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_NACKF) ) {			// Make sure ACK received
		for(uint8_t i=0; i<nbytes; i++) {
			while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_RXNE) );		// Wait for Rx to be ready
			data[i] = I2C1->RXDR;						// Read the data
		}
	}
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The acknowledge bits and the STOP are not written by this function.  It describes the whole transaction to the peripheral in advance, how many bytes (NBYTES) and what to do at the end (AUTOEND).  The I2C hardware puts them on the wire.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min. RD_WRN is the only new field — which is what put the device address on your screen twice. The register-level detail is in the ch-i2c Reference; match each piece to the trace as you go.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-break-it">
            <title>Part 6: Break It on Purpose</title>

            <!-- Prose otherwise WRITTEN 2026-08-30.  STILL OWED, blocked on
                 Q3: the debrief paragraph on the asymmetry the room saw —
                 no student-facing sentence may assert what the program
                 PRINTS on a wrong-address NACK until Petra confirms the
                 library's behavior (ground truth §2a, §9 Q3).  The trace
                 side (one transaction, NACK after the address) is verified
                 and may be asserted.  The "expected here" line is SPOKEN
                 (presenter note on the Part 6 commit slide), never
                 printed. -->

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-break-it">
                <title>Break it: a wrong device address</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>A NACK is easier to recognize the first time if you
                    produce one on purpose, on a bus you know is properly
                    configured.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-break-predict">
                    <statement><p>Change the <c>0x32</c> in
                    <c>LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL</c> to <c>0x30</c>.  Before you
                    run it, predict: what will CoolTerm show, and what will
                    the logic analyzer show?  Write both predictions
                    down.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-break-capture">
                    <statement><p>Rebuild and upload; then arm a single sweep
                    on the analyzer (trigger on DIO0 falling) and press the
                    board's reset button to catch the first transaction after
                    startup.  The trace stays blank until you press reset.
                    That is the sweep waiting, not a fault.  Read the decoded
                    row: what happened after the address byte?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-break-restore">
                    <statement><p>Change the address back to <c>0x32</c>,
                    rebuild, and confirm the link works again before we move
                    on.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

<!-- Gate 2' item 26: the old ins-day13-break-it block projected almost
     nothing entry 30's caption did not (in-the-room #2), and check_deck
     requires every <instructor> block to be projected — so the block is
     gone and its two load-bearing notes live in sl-day13-capture-a's
     presenter note below.  The wire-side answer IS the projected
     capture. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture-nack">
                <caption>The same read attempted at the wrong device address.
                One transaction: <c>h18 WR</c> goes out, no device answers,
                the acknowledge bit stays HIGH (the decoder prints
                <c>N</c>), and a STOP ends the transaction.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_a.png" width="97%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-capture-a">
    <ul>
        <li>This is the second kind of NACK, the one from nobody at all: no device recognized the address, so nothing pulled SDA LOW at the acknowledge bit.</li>
        <li>CoolTerm: compare what your board printed with what your neighbors' did.</li>
    </ul>
    <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_a.png" width="88%"/>
    <caption>One transaction: h18 WR goes out, nothing pulls SDA LOW at the acknowledge bit, and the decoder's P (a STOP) ends it.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min, the projected rescue for this capture too, and the reveal — debrief on it. h18 is 0x30 &gt;&gt; 1. Collect what the room's CoolTerm windows showed rather than asserting it — the library's wrong-address behavior is still being checked (Q3), and nothing student-facing may claim what the program prints here. (Day 10 Part 9 and the ch-i2c Reference already give the wire-and-program story from the Reference Manual §23.4.9; Petra is re-checking it, and any change lands in ch-i2c too.)</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-break-stretch">
                <title>If you finish early: a wrong register address</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-stretch-register">
                    <statement><p>Restore the device address, and this time
                    read a wrong <em>register</em>: call
                    <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(0x20)</c> instead of the
                    WHO_AM_I address.  Predict first: will the transactions
                    complete this time?  What value comes back?  Then capture
                    it, and check the value you got against the LSM303AGR
                    datasheet, §8.6 (CTRL_REG1_A).</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-stretch-display">
                    <statement><p>One more prediction: the display on your
                    bus answers to address 0x70 (already the 7-bit address,
                    no shift).  If you sent 0x0F to
                    <em>it</em> as a register address, the way this program
                    does to the accelerometer, what would happen at the
                    acknowledge bit after the address byte, and can the
                    value that comes back verify the link the way 0x33
                    does?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13-break-stretch">
                <p>With the right device address and a wrong register
                address, everything the accelerometer is asked to
                acknowledge, it acknowledges: it is being addressed
                correctly, so both transactions complete, and it returns
                whatever register 0x20 holds.  At power-on that is
                0x07 (CTRL_REG1_A's default, 0b00000111, straight from the
                datasheet's §8.6), so the wire shows a perfectly healthy read
                of a value that is simply not 0x33; the captured trace below
                shows exactly this.</p>
                <p>For the display question: the address 0x70 gets an ACK
                (the display is on the bus and recognizes it), and that ACK
                tells you that you reached <em>a</em> device.  Whether the
                read then completes, and what byte comes back, is
                discoverable on their own bus; let whoever captures it report
                it.  The WHO_AM_I value is what verifies that you reached the
                <em>right</em> device, and the HT16K33 has no such
                register.</p>
            </instructor>

<!-- CTRL_REG1_A named here, ahead of Part 7's register map, is INTENDED
     mystery-then-explain — Part 7's "You saw one of those defaults
     already" is the tie-back (Gate 2' continuity #3, confirmed). -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture-wrong-register">
                <caption>The right device address, the wrong register
                address: reading register 0x20 instead of WHO_AM_I.  Both
                transactions complete (every acknowledge the accelerometer
                owes is an ACK, and the read ends with the controller's own
                NACK, exactly as the WHO_AM_I read did), and the value that
                comes back is <c>h07</c>: CTRL_REG1_A's power-on default,
                0b00000111 (Datasheet §8.6).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_b.png" width="97%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-whoami-capture-wrong-register-closeup">
                <caption>The same wrong-register capture, close up: the write
                transaction delivering register address 0x20 (top), then the
                read transaction returning 0x07 (bottom).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_b_write.png" width="97%"/>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/waveforms_capture_b_read.png" width="76%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-capture-b" ref="fig-whoami-capture-wrong-register-closeup">
    <caption>Right device, wrong register: everything the accelerometer is asked to acknowledge, it acknowledges, and the value that comes back is h07, CTRL_REG1_A's power-on default.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The stretch's reveal — or the instructor demo of capture B if the stretch tier was cut for time. A healthy bus, reading the wrong thing perfectly. The whole-strip view is the figure above this one in the book.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 7 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13-driver-begins">
            <title>Part 7: The Accelerometer's Device Driver</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-30: the owed layers/second-verse
                 framing lives in the recipe paragraph ("the same steps you
                 followed for the display on Day 10", fig-firmware-layers by
                 xref only — never a second xml:id) and the closing
                 one-running-one-homework paragraph. -->

            <p>
                Writing a device driver follows five steps, the same
                steps you followed for the display on Day 10.
                <xref ref="fig-firmware-layers"/> is the map: the driver
                reaches the device only through the I2C library, and the
                application reaches the device only through the driver.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><p>From the datasheet, understand the basic I2C transfers
                for reading and writing for the device you want to talk
                to.</p></li>
                <li><p>From the datasheet, <c>#define</c> the device's
                addresses and commands.</p></li>
                <li><p>Define functions for the actions you need from the
                device.</p></li>
                <li><p>Write those functions using the <c>i2c.c</c> library
                functions.</p></li>
                <li><p>Devise unit tests to confirm the driver works.</p></li>
            </ol>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-layers" ref="fig-firmware-layers">
    <caption>The firmware layers from Day 10: the driver reaches the device only through the I2C library, and the application only through the driver.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Re-shown, not re-taught. The display's driver was the first pass through this map; this week you'll build the second — the difference is only which device is at the bottom.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-recipe">
    <p>Writing a device driver follows five steps, the same steps you followed for the display:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>From the datasheet, understand the basic I2C transfers for reading and writing for the device you want to talk to.</li>
        <li>From the datasheet, <c>#define</c> the device's addresses and commands.</li>
        <li>Define functions for the actions you need from the device.</li>
        <li>Write those functions using the <c>i2c.c</c> library functions.</li>
        <li>Devise unit tests to confirm the driver works.</li>
    </ol>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Her slide 28. Steps 1–3 are already done for this device — the header is next, and it is step 2 made visible.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                Step 2 is already done, and here is where it came from.  Open
                the <url href="external/datasheets/lsm303agr.pdf">LSM303AGR
                datasheet</url> to §7, Table 26
                (<em>Register address map</em>, p. 43), and put
                <c>lsm303agr.h</c> beside it: the header <em>is</em> that
                table, transcribed into <c>#define</c>s.  Here is the top of
                its register block:
            </p>

            <!-- The header excerpts below match assets/starters/lsm303agr.h
                 in CODE (the file is not registered in check_starters.py, so
                 the guard is this comment): two of her comments are elided —
                 "(Sec 6, 7.1)" after "Accelerometer registers" and
                 "Section 7.1 of" before "the datasheet" — because both cite
                 a stale datasheet revision (the hosted PDF's map is §7,
                 Table 26; ground truth §2) — and the WHO_AM_I_A line's tab
                 whitespace is normalized to spaces so the DEFAULT/TYPE
                 columns line up (Petra, 2026-08-30).  Do not restore
                 either. -->

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// I2C address for the sensor
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL    (0x32 >> 1)         // 0011001x

// ACCELEROMETER SECTION
// Accelerometer registers                // DEFAULT    TYPE
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A          0x0F   // 00110011   r
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A         0x20   // 00000111   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG2_A         0x21   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG3_A         0x22   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A         0x23   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG5_A         0x24   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG6_A         0x25   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_REFERENCE_A         0x26   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_STATUS_REG_A        0x27   // 00000000   r
#define LSM303_OUT_X_L_A           0x28
#define LSM303_OUT_X_H_A           0x29
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_L_A           0x2A
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_H_A           0x2B
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_L_A           0x2C
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_H_A           0x2D
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The real file keeps going (FIFO, interrupt, and click
                registers up to address 0x3D), and we leave all of those at
                their defaults.  The <c>_A</c> in every name marks the
                accelerometer's half of the register map; the magnetometer
                has its own, which we don't touch.  Notice the comment
                columns: for each control and status register, the header
                records the power-on default from the datasheet's table.  You
                saw one of those defaults already if you did the
                wrong-register capture.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-header">
    <p>Open the accelerometer datasheet to §7, Table 26 (the register address map), and put <c>lsm303agr.h</c> beside it: the header is that table, transcribed into <c>#define</c>s.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL    (0x32 >> 1)         // 0011001x

// Accelerometer registers                // DEFAULT    TYPE
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A          0x0F   // 00110011   r
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A         0x20   // 00000111   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG2_A         0x21   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG3_A         0x22   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A         0x23   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG5_A         0x24   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG6_A         0x25   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_REFERENCE_A         0x26   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_STATUS_REG_A        0x27   // 00000000   r
#define LSM303_OUT_X_L_A           0x28
#define LSM303_OUT_X_H_A           0x29
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_L_A           0x2A
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_H_A           0x2B
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_L_A           0x2C
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_H_A           0x2D
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 2 min. The real file keeps going — FIFO, interrupt and click registers up to 0x3D, all left at their defaults. Point at the DEFAULT column: the wrong-register capture read one of these defaults off the wire. _A is the accelerometer half; the magnetometer has its own.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13-header-tour">
                <title>Check the header against the datasheet</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-header-download">
                    <statement><p>Download <c>lsm303agr.h</c> from Canvas and
                    put it in your <c>mylib</c> folder.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13-header-check">
                    <statement><p>In the datasheet's Table 26 (p. 43), find
                    the rows for WHO_AM_I_A and CTRL_REG1_A and check them
                    against the header's <c>#define</c>s, the address and
                    the default value of each.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <p>
                Step 3 is also done: the header declares four functions, and
                they are the interface we'll build on for the rest of the
                week.
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// The accelerometer has several gain and update rate settings.  These are described
// in the datasheet and are set up in the AccelInit function.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelInit();

// The AccelRegisterWrite function should take a register address and a command byte
// to be written into the register.
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data);

// The AccelRegisterRead function should take a register address and return a data byte
// read from the register.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

// Here is the higher-level function to read the acceleration vector.  Output struct
// contains raw (unscaled) acceleration values.
void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s * const result);
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                One of the four is already running on your board.  We used
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c> to read the WHO_AM_I
                register.  Writing <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> is
                tonight's homework.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13-prototypes">
    <p>The header also declares the four functions of the driver, the interface we'll build on for the rest of the week.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// The accelerometer has several gain and update rate settings.  These are described
// in the datasheet and are set up in the AccelInit function.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelInit();

// The AccelRegisterWrite function should take a register address and a command byte
// to be written into the register.
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data);

// The AccelRegisterRead function should take a register address and return a data byte
// read from the register.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

// Here is the higher-level function to read the acceleration vector.  Output struct
// contains raw (unscaled) acceleration values.
void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s * const result);
]]></code></program>
    <caption>One of the four is already running.  We used RegisterRead to read the WHO_AM_I register.  RegisterWrite is tonight's homework, on paper.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Do not open AccelInit or ReadRaw beyond their comments — the settings are the homework's scavenger hunt, and ReadRaw's walk comes with the data format.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

