    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================
         Day 13x In-Class.  Week 7 session 3 (plans/day13x-prompt.md)
         fills this section per plans/day13x.md: the proof-mass physics,
         MEMS, the datasheet characteristics table, the data format
         (two's complement is FIRST TEACHING here), auto-increment and
         byte assembly, the control registers.  x-day: no reading.

         PARKED below (commented out): the salvageable pieces of the
         rough chapter, none of them trusted (ground truth §7).  Figures
         are rebuilt per the manifest (ground truth §6), not reused as
         extracted.
         ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-accel-day13x">
        <title>Day 13x In-Class: What a MEMS Accelerometer Is</title>

        <introduction>
            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-31, from the slides Petra passed. -->
            <p>
                Yesterday we wired the LSM303AGR onto the I2C bus, read
                0x33 out of its WHO_AM_I register, and watched the
                transactions on the logic analyzer.  Today we'll look at
                how the sensor works inside: the physics that senses
                acceleration, the different configurations in its
                datasheet, and the format in which the acceleration data
                is sent from the accelerometer to us.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-physics">
            <title>Part 1: The Physics of a Proof Mass</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-31.  The level-app hook lives in
                 act-day13x-mass-commit's introduction and is not
                 repeated; the C1/C2 differential detail is Reference
                 material (sec-accel-reference) and the instructor slide —
                 do not grow it back here. -->
            <p>
                An accelerometer has to turn a physical acceleration into
                something a circuit can measure.  We'll follow that chain
                in three steps: acceleration to displacement, displacement
                to capacitance, and capacitance to a number the chip can
                send us.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13x-mass-commit">
                <title>What does the mass do?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Your phone actually has an accelerometer built into
                    it: it is how the phone knows whether you are holding
                    it upright or sideways, and turns the screen to match.
                    Today we'll look at how it does that.  Inside the sensor
                    a small mass, the <term>proof mass</term>, is connected
                    by a spring to the sensor's case.</p>
                    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mass_spring_rest.svg" width="60%"/>
                    <p>Suppose the case accelerates to the right.
                    Seen from inside the case, what does the mass do?
                    Discuss at your table.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>

            <!-- The projected form of act-day13x-mass-commit: the same
                 question with the AT-REST drawing on the wall (the
                 displaced drawing is the reveal's — P-15).  Two tellings
                 of one question: change them together. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-mass-commit" room="yes">
    <p>Your phone actually has an accelerometer built into it: it is how the phone knows whether you are holding it upright or sideways, and turns the screen to match.  Inside the sensor that does this, a small mass, the <term>proof mass</term>, is connected by a spring to the sensor's case.  Suppose the case accelerates to the right.  Seen from inside the case, what does the mass do?  Discuss at your table.</p>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mass_spring_rest.svg" width="56%"/>
    <note>≈ 3 min, discussed at the tables.  The phone hook is spoken, not performed — everyone has seen their screen rotate; do not make the room try it.  Then pose the mass question.  Collect two or three answers out loud without ruling on them; the derivation on the next slides settles it, not you.  The drawing is deliberately at rest — the displaced drawing is the reveal's.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13x-mass-commit">
                <p>Because of its inertia, the mass gets left behind: seen
                from inside the case, it moves backward, opposite the
                acceleration, and the spring stretches until the spring
                force matches the force needed to carry the mass along with
                the case.  That displacement is what is measured.  How far the
                spring stretches, and what it has to do with the size of the
                acceleration, is the derivation we do next.</p>
            </instructor>

            <p>
                Because of its inertia, the mass resists the case's
                acceleration: seen from inside the case it is displaced
                backward, opposite the acceleration, until the spring's
                restoring force grows to match the force needed to carry
                it along.  That displacement is what is measured, and we
                can turn that observation into a formula
                (<xref ref="fig-accel-proof-mass"/>).  Two familiar facts
                are enough:
                acceleration <m>a</m> results in force <m>ma</m> on the
                mass, and the spring counteracts with force <m>kx</m>,
                where <m>k</m> is the spring constant and <m>x</m> is the
                displacement of the mass.  Measure the displacement when
                the forces balance, and we can calculate the acceleration.
                One spring measures acceleration along its own length
                only, so a 3-axis accelerometer replicates the system
                along three axes.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-proof-mass">
                <caption>A proof mass <m>m</m> connected by a spring (spring
                constant <m>k</m>) to the sensor's case.  When the case
                accelerates, the mass is displaced by <m>x</m> from its rest
                position; at balance the spring force <m>kx</m> equals the
                force <m>ma</m> that accelerates the mass with the case, so
                measuring <m>x</m> gives the acceleration
                <m>a = (k/m)\,x</m>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mass_spring.svg" width="72%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-derivation" ref="fig-accel-proof-mass">
    <ul>
        <li>Acceleration <em>a</em> results in force <em>ma</em> on the mass.</li>
        <li>The spring counteracts with force <em>kx</em>, where <em>k</em> is the spring constant and <em>x</em> is the displacement of the mass.</li>
        <li>Measure the displacement <em>x</em> when the forces balance: <em>kx</em> = <em>ma</em>.  We can then calculate <em>a</em> = (<em>k</em>/<em>m</em>)<em>x</em>: if we observe a displacement <em>x</em>, then the mass has undergone acceleration <em>a</em>.</li>
        <li>This system only measures acceleration along the length of the spring.  For a 3-axis accelerometer, replicate it along three axes.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 4 min, the reveal's derivation — her slide 3, two named facts before they are combined. If we can measure the displacement we can compute acceleration; the whole rest of today is the two hand-offs that follow (displacement to capacitance, capacitance to bits). No physics course is assumed — both facts are named on the slide.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                There are multiple ways of measuring a displacement:
                resistive, capacitive, and inductive techniques.  Our
                sensor uses the capacitive one.  One capacitor plate is attached to the mass and one
                to the case (<xref ref="fig-accel-capacitive"/>): as the
                mass moves, the gap between the plates changes, the
                capacitance changes with it, and measuring the capacitance
                lets us compute the displacement.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-capacitive">
                <caption>Turning displacement into something we can measure
                electrically: one capacitor plate is attached to the mass
                and one to the case.  As the mass moves, the gap between
                the plates changes, and the capacitance changes with
                it.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/capacitive_pickoff.svg" width="62%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-capacitive" ref="fig-accel-capacitive">
    <ul>
        <li>There are multiple ways of measuring a displacement: resistive, capacitive, and inductive techniques.  Our sensor uses the capacitive one.</li>
        <li>Imagine capacitor plates attached to the mass and to the case: as the mass moves, the gap changes, and measuring the capacitance lets us compute <m>x</m>.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 4. The chain so far: acceleration → displacement → capacitance. One hand-off left: capacitance → a number.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                In a <term>MEMS</term> accelerometer, the proof mass, the
                springs, and the capacitor plates are all machined out of
                silicon, on the scale of µm
                (<xref ref="fig-accel-mems"/>).  MEMS stands for
                Micro-Electro-Mechanical System.  A capacitance-to-voltage
                circuit on the same chip translates the changing
                capacitance into a voltage signal.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-mems">
                <caption>The whole mass-spring-capacitor system, machined
                out of silicon.  Left: the mechanism, at rest and under
                acceleration.  The moving plate is the proof mass,
                suspended on springs, and its fingers interleave with
                fixed fingers to form capacitors that change as it moves.
                In the accelerating panel, the case and its fixed plates
                accelerate to the right, and the moving plate (the mass)
                gets left behind, as we derived.
                Right: a micrograph of a real device's interleaved
                fingers, a few µm across (surface micromachined
                capacitors: Roger Howe, Stanford).</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="66% 24%" margins="2%" valign="middle">
                    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mems_mechanism.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/mems_micrograph.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-phone-video">
                <caption>The whole chain we just built, animated: how a
                smartphone knows up from down.  Worth five minutes if
                you'd like to see the proof mass and its capacitors in
                motion.</caption>
                <video youtube="KZVgKu6v808" width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-mems" ref="fig-accel-mems" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>This is a MEMS accelerometer, a Micro-Electro-Mechanical System: the proof mass, the springs, and the capacitor plates are all machined out of silicon, on the scale of µm.</li>
        <li>A capacitance-to-voltage circuit on the same chip translates the changing capacitance into a voltage signal.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 5 — the innovation of the last thirty years is putting the whole mass-spring system into silicon. The left panel is at rest; the right panel shows the plate displaced under acceleration. The accelerating panel now says it itself: the case and its fixed plates accelerate right, and the mass gets left behind. The differential C1/C2 detail is the instructor slide that follows.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-mems-differential" instructor="yes">
    <p>Here is what is inside the chip:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>A proof mass is suspended by tiny springs, and on both sides are interdigitated comb fingers.  These form two capacitors, C_1 and C_2.</li>
        <li>When acceleration occurs, the mass moves slightly: one capacitance increases and the other decreases, so the system measures the difference, ΔC = C_1 − C_2.</li>
        <li>Since Q = CV, if C_1 increases and C_2 decreases the differential charge changes: ΔQ = (C_1 − C_2) · V_exc.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Optional aside — show if the differential question comes up; no budgeted time. The written C1/C2 detail also goes to the chapter's Reference section.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-applications">
            <title>Part 2: Accelerometers Are Everywhere</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-31, carrying exactly what
                 sl-day13x-applications carries. -->
            <p>
                Tilt is the case we started with: your phone measures
                gravity's pull along each axis to decide when to rotate
                its screen.  An airbag sensor detects the sudden
                deceleration of a crash.  Before solid-state drives, an
                accelerometer detected a falling laptop and pulled the
                disk heads off the spinning drive before it hit the
                floor.  A step counter in a wearable picks up the impact
                of every footfall, and football helmets carry
                accelerometers to measure hits to the head.
                Accelerometers also monitor machinery: a change in a
                machine's vibration pattern can flag a failure building
                before anything else shows it.  Paired with a gyroscope
                they form an IMU (inertial measurement unit), the motion
                sensor in vehicles and drones.  Two local companies
                founded by Thayer alumni build on exactly this
                technology: SignalQuest (precision motion sensing) and
                Simbex (the helmet impact sensors).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-gyro-video">
                <caption>The gyroscope is the accelerometer's partner in
                an IMU, and it is built with the same MEMS technology: a
                vibrating mass, and a capacitive pickoff for the tiny
                Coriolis displacement that rotation produces.  This video
                shows how.</caption>
                <video youtube="XsjvaYAFN1M" width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-applications">
    <ul>
        <li>Tilt: your phone measures gravity's pull along each axis to decide when to rotate its screen.</li>
        <li>Shock: an airbag sensor detects the sudden deceleration of a crash.</li>
        <li>Free fall: before solid-state drives, an accelerometer detected a falling laptop and pulled the disk heads off the spinning drive before it hit the floor.</li>
        <li>Wearables: a step counter picks up the impact of every footfall, and football helmets carry accelerometers to measure hits to the head.</li>
        <li>Vibration: accelerometers monitor machinery, since a change in a machine's vibration pattern can flag a failure building before anything else shows it.</li>
        <li>Paired with a gyroscope they form an IMU (inertial measurement unit), the motion sensor in vehicles and drones.</li>
        <li>Two local companies founded by Thayer alumni build on exactly this technology: SignalQuest (precision motion sensing) and Simbex (the helmet impact sensors).</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min, her slide 6 (a minute moved to Part 4's ReadRaw beat at Gate 3'). If the clock is behind, this Part drops to one example. The gyroscope aside is the instructor slide that follows.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-gyro" instructor="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Inside a MEMS gyroscope, a tiny mass vibrates.  If the chip rotates, the vibrating mass feels a sideways Coriolis force, and that tiny perpendicular displacement is measured capacitively, with the same technology as the accelerometer.</li>
        <li>The gyroscope picks up twisting rather than shifting; together the pair gives a real-time six-dimensional motion sensor.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Optional aside — show if the gyroscope question comes up; no budgeted time. Her slide-6 speaker note.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-datasheet">
            <title>Part 3: The LSM303AGR, and Its Datasheet</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-31, from the passed slides (her
                 pass 1 struck "from earlier today" on the charge-amplifier
                 parenthetical — keep it "(a capacitance-to-voltage
                 circuit)"). -->
            <p>
                Our device is the STMicro LSM303AGR, the chip whose
                WHO_AM_I register we read yesterday.  It carries a 3-axis
                accelerometer, a 3-axis magnetometer, and a temperature
                sensor; we'll only use the accelerometer.  Inside it
                (<xref ref="fig-lsm303agr-block"/>), the signal follows
                the path we just discussed: a pair of variable capacitors
                per axis, then, through a multiplexer, one charge
                amplifier (a capacitance-to-voltage circuit) and one
                analog-to-digital converter shared by all three axes, and
                control logic that puts the result on the I2C bus.  You
                interact with the chip through its control and data
                registers, over I2C (SPI is the other option, and the
                breakout wires it for I2C by default), and through
                hardware interrupt pins we're not using.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-lsm303agr-block">
                <caption>The LSM303AGR's block diagram, from its datasheet.
                The accelerometer is the upper half, boxed in blue: six
                sense capacitors, a pair per axis (X±, Y±, Z±), feed a
                multiplexer, and the three axes share one charge amplifier
                and one analog-to-digital converter behind it, the same
                architecture as the STM32C031C6's ADC
                (<xref ref="ch-adc"/>), which likewise multiplexes
                several input channels through one shared converter.  Control logic answers on
                the SCL and SDA pins, boxed in red (the datasheet prints
                them SCL/SPC and SDA/SDI/SDO, because the same pins serve
                SPI).  The lower half is a magnetometer, which we don't
                use; the chip also contains a temperature sensor.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/lsm303agr_block.svg" width="88%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-block">
    <ul>
        <li>Our device is the STMicro LSM303AGR: a 3-axis accelerometer, a 3-axis magnetometer, and a temperature sensor.  We'll only use the accelerometer.</li>
        <li>The signal follows the path we just discussed: a pair of variable capacitors per axis, then, through a multiplexer, one charge amplifier (a capacitance-to-voltage circuit) and one analog-to-digital converter shared by all three axes, and control logic that puts the result on the I2C bus.</li>
        <li>You interact with it through control and data registers, over I2C (SPI is the other option; the breakout wires it for I2C by default), and through hardware interrupt pins we're not using.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slides 7 and 10, folded — the claims first; the diagram is next, full size, and the chain gets walked there. Registers over I2C is yesterday made general: WHO_AM_I is one you read; the control registers are the ones you write. If someone asks about the interrupt pins: you would wire one to a pin on your microcontroller, set up a pin-change interrupt, and write an ISR for it — the GPIO interrupts chapter.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-block-fig" ref="fig-lsm303agr-block">
    <caption>The accelerometer is the boxed upper half: six sense capacitors, a pair per axis, into the multiplexer, and the one charge amplifier and A/D converter they share.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Walk the chain left to right on the diagram — this is Part 1's physics arriving at yesterday's bus. The whole diagram is shown, magnetometer included, so the room can see the two halves sharing one I2C/SPI interface.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The next number to take from the datasheet is the size of
                one step of the converter's output, and we can
                derive it before we read it off the table.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13x-sensitivity">
                <title>Derive the sensitivity</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>The datasheet's sensor-characteristics table
                    (<url href="external/datasheets/lsm303agr.pdf">LSM303AGR
                    datasheet</url>, §2.1, Table 3, pp. 13–14) says how
                    acceleration at the input is turned into bits at the
                    output.  The sensor's A/D converter turns a continuous
                    signal into a digital one, and the datasheet lets us
                    choose how many bits we get (8, 10, or 12) and the
                    measurement range: ±2 g, ±4 g, ±8 g, or ±16 g, where
                    1 g is the acceleration of gravity, 9.8 m/s², and one
                    milli-g (mg) is a thousandth of that.  We'll use ±2 g,
                    and the sensor's <term>normal</term> resolution mode,
                    which gives 10 valid bits per reading.</p>
                    <p>The <term>sensitivity</term> is the size of
                    one step of the output: how much acceleration one least
                    significant bit stands for.  From the range and the bit
                    count, compute it, in milli-g per step.  This is the same
                    calculation we did for the ADC (<xref ref="ch-adc"/>):
                    the full range, divided into <m>2^{bits}</m>
                    bins.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-specs-commit" room="yes">
    <p>The sensor's A/D converter turns a continuous signal into a digital one, and the datasheet lets us choose how many bits we get (8, 10, or 12) and the measurement range, measured in units of g (gravity) rather than in volts or degrees.  The sensitivity is the size of one step: how much acceleration does one least significant bit stand for?  Compute it, in milli-g (mg), for the ±2 g range in normal mode, which gives 10 valid bits.  (This is the same calculation we did for the ADC on Day 7: the full range, divided into 2^bits bins.)</p>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_masked.svg" width="74%"/>
    <note>≈ 3 min, committed in writing before the reveal.  The sensitivity column is masked on purpose — the datasheet prints the answer, and the point is to derive it (range / 2^bits, Day 7's 1 LSB = V_ref/2^B in new units).  Full range 4 g, not 2: the range is ±2 g.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13x-sensitivity">
                <p>The measurement range is measured in units of g
                (gravity) rather than in volts or degrees, and it is the
                first thing to decide, so that you can select the correct
                setting.  Next is the sensitivity: how are bits at the
                output related to acceleration at the input?  The
                sensitivity is the full measurement range divided by
                2^bits, the resolution of the sensor.  2^bits is the
                number of bins the input range is divided into, and the
                width of each bin is the sensitivity.</p>
                <p>Here: the full range is 4 g (from −2 g to +2 g), and in
                normal mode there are 10 valid bits, so the range is
                divided into 2¹⁰ = 1024 bins: 4 g / 1024 = 3.90625 mg per
                step.  You can only pick up differences that are more than
                3.9 mg.  The datasheet's Table 3 prints 3.9 mg/LSB for this
                range and mode (our derived number, rounded), and the
                table's note 8 says the same thing in its own words:
                <q>1LSb = 3.9 mg in normal mode (10-bit) at FS=±2 g</q>
                (mg/LSB is milli-g per least significant bit).  Rounded
                once more, one digit of the output is worth about 4 mg.</p>
                <!-- Her slide-8 note said "the lower the number of bits,
                     the faster you can generate measurements"; the
                     datasheet (§8.6 Table 35) says normal and
                     high-resolution share one ODR list — only low-power
                     adds the faster rates.  The clause below is corrected
                     against the datasheet, not against her. -->
                <p>The accelerometer has three resolution modes: in
                low-power mode you only get 8 bits, in normal mode 10, in
                high-resolution 12.  Fewer bits means bigger steps, and
                low-power mode runs at output data rates the other two
                cannot (§8.6, Table 35).  Notice in the table how the
                sensitivity gets better as you go to higher
                resolutions.</p>
            </instructor>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-specs">
                <caption>The sensor-characteristics table's sensitivity and
                zero-g offset rows (LSM303AGR datasheet §2.1, Table 3,
                pp. 13–14).  Top: the full-scale range rows.  Middle: one
                sensitivity row per range and resolution mode
                (high-resolution is 12 bits, normal 10, low-power 8).  At
                ±2 g in normal mode the table prints 3.9 mg/LSB, milli-g
                per least significant bit, the size of one step: the
                number we derived as 4 g / 2¹⁰ = 3.90625 mg (the ±2 g
                range spans 4 g, from −2 g to +2 g).  Bottom: the
                zero-g offset row.  A sensor lying perfectly flat reads
                typically ±40 mg away from the true value, and the table's
                Min and Max columns allow ±80.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_table.svg" width="86%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-specs-reveal">
    <ul>
        <li>The unmasked table confirms the arithmetic.  The three modes trade resolution against speed: fewer bits means bigger steps, and low-power mode runs at rates the other two cannot.</li>
        <li>The zero-g offset row is below it: lying perfectly flat, the sensor reads typically ±40 mg away from the true value, and the datasheet's Min and Max columns allow ±80.  40 mg would be ten of our steps, 80 mg would be twenty, and a significant amount of error.</li>
    </ul>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/accel_specs_reveal.svg" width="64%"/>
    <note>≈ 1 min — the unmasked column confirms the arithmetic; then the modes trade and the offset row. ±40 mg typical is significant — it comes back as the stretch's calibration question, and tomorrow's suggested tests will show it on real boards.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The table in <xref ref="fig-accel-specs"/>
                confirms the arithmetic.  The three modes trade resolution
                against speed: fewer bits means bigger steps, and
                low-power mode runs at rates the other two cannot.  The
                zero-g offset row is below it: lying perfectly flat, the
                sensor reads typically ±40 mg away from the true value,
                and the datasheet's Min and Max columns allow ±80.  40 mg
                would be ten of our steps, 80 mg would be twenty, and a
                significant amount of error.
            </p>

            <p>
                The breakout board carries the parts the chip needs: the
                I2C pull-up resistors and a
                voltage regulator
                (<xref ref="fig-accel-breakout"/>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-breakout">
                <caption>The breakout board's schematic (partial).  The
                board carries what the chip needs: pull-up resistors on SDA
                and SCL, and a regulator to supply the chip.  The
                6-pin header brings power, ground, SCL, SDA and the
                magnetometer interrupt out to the breadboard.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/breakout_schematic.svg" width="92%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-breakout" ref="fig-accel-breakout">
    <caption>The breakout carries the parts the chip needs: the I2C pull-up resistors and a voltage regulator.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, one sentence — her slide 9.  The big chip is the accelerometer, the little one the regulator; the SDA and SCL wires run to on-board pull-ups.  Do not linger; the schematic is here so the board is not a black box.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4 — the CRUCIAL step (plans/day13x.md): staged one idea at
             a time; nobody meets left-justification, sign, and scaling in
             the same breath. -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-data-format">
            <title>Part 4: The Data Format</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-31, from the passed slides.  The
                 sign paragraphs are the only place a student can go back
                 to two's complement (slides are stripped from the reading
                 book) — do not compress them.  Her pass 2 fixed the sign
                 route: the top bit contributes its negative weight and
                 the remaining bits are read as usual; do NOT reintroduce
                 the unsigned-minus-2^width formulation. -->
            <p>
                The acceleration readings are put in the data registers of
                the accelerometer's memory, at a register address we can
                look up in the datasheet, which calls it a
                <term>sub-address</term> (SUB).  Each axis's reading
                is 16 bits wide (two bytes), and by default the reading
                is stored in them <term>left-justified</term>: the valid
                bits are at the top of the 2-byte word, and zeros fill in
                below.  In low-power mode, for example, there are 8 valid
                bits, so only the top byte actually carries relevant
                information.  Suppose the two bytes contain the reading
                0x4000 (<xref ref="fig-accel-one-byte"/>): the top byte
                is 0x40 = 64.  In low-power mode with a full-scale
                range of ±2 g, each step is 4 g / 2⁸ = 15.625 mg, so this
                reading corresponds to an acceleration of
                64 × 15.625 mg = 1000 mg: one g.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-one-byte">
                <caption>The reading 0x4000 as it sits in the two data
                registers, all 16 bits.  In low-power mode only the top
                byte carries valid bits (0x40 = 64), and the low byte
                is zeros: that is what left-justified means.  At ±2 g in
                low-power mode each step is 15.625 mg, so 64 steps is
                1000 mg, one g.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/one_byte_example.svg" width="76%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-one-byte">
    <ul>
        <li>The acceleration readings are put in the data registers of the accelerometer's memory, at a register address we can look up in the datasheet, which calls it a <term>sub-address</term> (SUB).</li>
        <li>Each axis's reading is 16 bits wide (two bytes), and by default the reading is stored in them <term>left-justified</term>: the valid bits are at the top of the 2-byte word, and zeros fill in below.</li>
        <li>Example: In low-power mode there are 8 valid bits, so only the top byte actually carries relevant information.  Suppose the two bytes contain the reading 0x4000: the top byte is 0x40 = 64.  In low-power mode with a full-scale range of ±2 g, each step is 4 g / 2⁸ = 15.625 mg, so this reading corresponds to an acceleration of 64 × 15.625 mg = 1000 mg: one g.</li>
    </ul>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/one_byte_example.svg" width="80%"/>
    <note>≈ 3 min, one idea at a time: where the readings live, then left-justified, then one byte unsigned — read the top byte, scale by the step size.  No sign yet and no three-mode picture yet, on purpose.  The picture shows 0x4000 as its 16 bits, top byte labeled.  0x4000 → +1000 mg is the number the flip example will mirror.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                So far we read the data as unsigned, but acceleration has
                a direction, and the sensor's output is therefore given as
                a <term>two's complement</term> number.  We have not
                needed this encoding until now, so here is a quick
                review: the top bit of a two's complement number is
                worth its usual amount made <em>negative</em>, and every
                other bit keeps its usual value.  The idea is easier to
                see in 4 bits (<xref ref="fig-accel-sign"/>): 0b0010
                corresponds to a decimal value of 2 regardless of whether
                or not we are in two's complement (since the top bit is
                0), whereas the number 0b1110 corresponds to
                −8 + 4 + 2 = −2, because the top bit is worth −8 and the
                rest are ordinary.  A number with its top bit set is
                therefore negative: the top bit contributes its negative
                weight, and the remaining bits are read as usual and add
                to it: 0b1110 is −8 + 0b110 = −8 + 6 = −2.  In our 16-bit
                readings the top bit is bit 15, worth −2¹⁵.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-sign">
                <caption>Two's complement, in 4 bits and in 16.  Left: the
                number 0b1110, read with its bit weights: the top weight
                is −8 rather than +8, so the value is −8 + 4 + 2 = −2.
                Right: in the sensor's 16-bit readings the same rule makes
                bit 15 worth −2¹⁵, and every other bit keeps its usual
                value.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/sign_bit_weights.svg" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-sign">
    <ul>
        <li>So far we read the data as unsigned, but acceleration has a direction, and the sensor's output is therefore given as a <term>two's complement</term> number: bit 15 is worth −2¹⁵, not +2¹⁵.  Every other bit keeps its usual value.</li>
        <li>The same idea is easier to see in 4 bits: 0b0010 corresponds to a decimal value of 2 regardless of whether or not we are in two's complement (since the top bit is 0), whereas the number 0b1110 corresponds to −8 + 4 + 2 = −2, because the top bit is worth −8 and the rest are ordinary.</li>
        <li>A number with its top bit set is therefore negative: the top bit contributes its negative weight, and the remaining bits are read as usual and add to it: 0b1110 is −8 + 0b110 = −8 + 6 = −2.</li>
    </ul>
    <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/sign_bit_weights.svg" width="64%"/>
    <note>≈ 3 min — FIRST teaching, not review: nothing before this week has decoded a signed bit pattern.  Land the one fact (the top bit's value is negative) and its consequence (negative top weight plus the remaining bits), both shown in 4 bits before anyone sees 16 — point at the crossed-out weight in the picture as you say it.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                We'll work the whole conversion once, on the reading
                0xC000, step by step.  Assume we are in low-power mode at
                a full-scale range of ±2 g.
                In binary the reading is 0b1100 0000 0000 0000, and bit 15 is
                set, so the value is negative.  The top
                byte carries the reading: 0xC0 = 0b1100 0000, whose top
                bit is worth −128 while the remaining bits are
                0b100 0000 = 64, so the value is −128 + 64 = −64.  Scaling
                by the step size gives −64 × 15.625 mg = −1000 mg: minus
                one g, comfortably inside the ±2000 mg the range allows.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-worked">
    <p>The reading is 0xC000.  Assume we are in low-power mode at a full-scale range of ±2 g.  We convert it step by step:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Write the binary: 0xC000 = 0b1100 0000 0000 0000.  Bit 15 is set, so the value is negative.</li>
        <li>Read the top byte (low-power mode): 0xC0 = 0b1100 0000.  The top bit is worth −128, and the remaining bits are 0b100 0000 = 64, so the value is −128 + 64 = −64.</li>
        <li>Scale by the step size: −64 × 15.625 mg = <em>−1000 mg</em>: minus one g, comfortably inside the ±2000 mg the range allows.</li>
    </ol>
    <note>≈ 2 min, worked TOGETHER on the board, top to bottom — this is the model for the conversion the room does alone shortly.  The sign step is the previous slide's rule applied at 8 bits: negative top-bit weight plus the remaining bits.  The plausibility check is said out loud.  The next slide's formula gets the same answer from the whole 16-bit word.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The other two modes work the same way.
                <xref ref="fig-accel-data-format"/> shows all three
                writing their valid bits into the same 16-bit word, with
                one equation per mode.  To convert a raw reading, shift
                the zero bits off the bottom, then multiply by that
                mode's step size.  Every one of the three simplifies to
                the same thing:
                <m>a = 4g \cdot \mathrm{raw} / 2^{16}</m>.  This works
                because the valid bits always start at bit 15, so the
                shift and the step size always cancel to 2¹⁶.  The same
                reading decoded this way gives the same answer: as a
                whole 16-bit word, 0xC000 is −32768 + 16384 = −16384,
                and 4 g × (−16384) / 2¹⁶ = −1000 mg, the number the
                top-byte route gave.  When no mode is stated, this is
                the route to use: the whole 16-bit word, with the
                collapsed formula.  Two things to keep in mind.  The 4 g
                in this formula is the ±2 g range: choose a different
                full-scale range and the constant changes with it, so it
                is not always the same formula.  And if we store the raw
                reading in a signed C data type such as <c>int16_t</c>
                (an integer, not an unsigned), then the whole two's
                complement interpretation is automatically taken care
                of.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-data-format">
                <caption>The three resolution modes write their valid bits
                into the same 16-bit word, always starting at bit 15:
                low-power mode fills 8 bits, normal 10, high-resolution 12,
                with zeros below.  Bit 15 is the sign, worth −2¹⁵, so one
                formula converts the raw word in every mode:
                <m>a = \mathrm{FS} \cdot \mathrm{raw} / 2^{16}</m>, where
                FS is the full-scale range, 4 g here.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13x-Accelerometer/data_format_rows.svg" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-collapse" ref="fig-accel-data-format" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>To convert a raw reading, shift the zero bits off the bottom, then multiply by that mode's step size.  The three equations in the figure do exactly this, one per mode.</li>
        <li>Every one of them simplifies to the same thing: a = 4g · raw / 2¹⁶.  This works because the valid bits always start at bit 15, so the shift and the step size always cancel to 2¹⁶.  (The 4 g is the ±2 g range: a different full-scale range changes the constant.)</li>
        <li>If we store the raw reading in a signed C data type such as <c>int16_t</c> (an integer, not an unsigned), the two's complement interpretation is automatically taken care of.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Her slide 15's three bit-rows, with the zeros visible and each mode's equation at the end of its own row — show the algebra on one row and say the other two collapse the same way.  Close the loop on 0xC000: 4 g × (−16384) / 2¹⁶ = −1000 mg, the same answer the top-byte route gave.  The punchline is one formula in every mode.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                The units of the sensor's output are g (1 g = 9.8 m/s²),
                so multiplying by 1000 gives milli-g, and for the ±2 g
                range the conversion is
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
a = (4 * ACCEL_raw * 1000) >> 16;              // in mg

// or, with names instead of magic numbers:
#define ACC_FS            4                    // full-scale range, in g
#define ACC_REGISTERWIDTH 16
#define MILLI             1000

a = (ACC_FS * ACCEL_raw * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The shift right by 16 does the divide by 2¹⁶ (shifting a
                signed value has one subtlety, which
                <xref ref="sec-accel-reference"/> spells out), and the
                <c>#define</c> names are the ones tomorrow's test program,
                <c>accel_test.c</c>, uses.  Note that <c>a</c> here is
                a signed integer, not an unsigned type, so the sign
                survives the arithmetic.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-mg-formula">
    <p>In milli-g, for the ±2 g range, the conversion is</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
a = (4 * ACCEL_raw * 1000) >> 16;              // in mg

// or, with names instead of magic numbers:
#define ACC_FS            4                    // full-scale range, in g
#define ACC_REGISTERWIDTH 16
#define MILLI             1000

a = (ACC_FS * ACCEL_raw * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The units of the output are g (1 g = 9.8 m/s²), so multiplying by 1000 gives milli-g, and the shift by 16 does the divide by 2¹⁶.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, her slide 16.  The #defines are accel_test.c's, verbatim; tomorrow's scaling line in accel_test.c is this computation, done once per axis.</note>
</slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day13x-convert">
                <title>Convert a reading</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day13x-convert">
                    <statement><p>The sensor is in low-power mode on the
                    ±2 g range, and the
                    raw reading is 0xE000.  On paper, convert it to
                    milli-g: write out the binary, decide the sign, compute
                    the value, and carry it to mg.  Then check your answer
                    for plausibility: is it inside the range?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day13x-offset-stretch">
                    <statement><p>If you finish early: the datasheet's
                    zero-g offset row says a flat, motionless board
                    typically reads ±40 mg off, and its Min and Max columns
                    allow ±80.  Suppose your board averages +1032 mg lying
                    flat and −968 mg lying flipped.  What is
                    its offset, and how would you correct future
                    readings?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day13x-convert">
                <p>In low-power mode the top byte carries the reading:
                0xE0 = 0b1110 0000.  The top bit is worth −128, and the
                remaining bits are 0b110 0000 = 96, so the value is
                −128 + 96 = −32.  Scaling by the step size:
                −32 × 15.625 mg = <em>−500 mg</em>.  Plausible, well
                inside ±2000 mg.  (The 16-bit route agrees: bit 15 is
                worth −32768, the remaining bits are 24576, the value is
                −8192, and (4 × −8192 × 1000) / 65536 = −500 mg.)</p>
                <p>The stretch: the two readings should be +1000 and
                −1000 mg; they are both 32 mg high, so the offset is their
                average, (1032 + (−968)) / 2 = +32 mg.  Correct future
                readings by subtracting 32 mg.  That is how you calibrate
                the zero-g offset out: read flat, read flipped, and
                average the two readings.</p>
            </instructor>

            <!-- Auto-increment: the load-bearing quote is written now
                 (the slide asserts it); B-8a. -->
            <p>
                The accelerations along the three axes arrive in six data
                registers at successive sub-addresses, <c>OUT_X_L_A</c>
                at 0x28 through <c>OUT_Z_H_A</c> at 0x2D, and you can
                read all six bytes
                in one transfer, starting at 0x28, if you set one bit in
                the register address first.  The datasheet explains that
                bit in §6.1.1 (p. 38), where its <q>slave</q> is our
                target, and <q>LSb</q> and <q>MSb</q> are the least and
                most significant bits:
            </p>
            <blockquote>
                <p>After the start condition (ST) a slave address is sent,
                once a slave acknowledge (SAK) has been returned, an 8-bit
                sub-address (SUB) is transmitted: the 7 LSb represent the
                actual register address while the MSB enables address auto
                increment.  If the MSb of the SUB field is <sq>1</sq>, the
                SUB (register address) is automatically increased to allow
                multiple data read/writes.</p>
            </blockquote>
            <p>
                So if we want to read multiple registers successively in
                one transaction, starting at 0x28 (where the low byte of
                the x-acceleration is stored), we need to use
                <c>0x28 | (1&lt;&lt;7)</c> for the register address from
                which to start reading.  The accelerometer then advances
                to the next data register after every byte.  This is
                Table 23 of <xref ref="fig-i2c-transfer-pattern"/>, the
                multi-byte read: the controller acknowledges each data
                byte (MAK) so the target serves the next register, and a
                final not-acknowledge (NMAK) ends the transfer.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-autoincrement">
    <p>The accelerations along the three axes arrive in six data registers at successive sub-addresses, listed in <c>lsm303agr.h</c>, and you can read all six bytes in one transfer, starting at 0x28.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LSM303_OUT_X_L_A           0x28
#define LSM303_OUT_X_H_A           0x29
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_L_A           0x2A
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_H_A           0x2B
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_L_A           0x2C
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_H_A           0x2D
]]></code></program>
    <p>But note the datasheet's own wording (§6.1.1), where <q>LSb</q> and <q>MSb</q> are the least and most significant bits: <q>an 8-bit sub-address (SUB) is transmitted: the 7 LSb represent the actual register address while the MSB enables address auto increment.  If the MSb of the SUB field is <sq>1</sq>, the SUB (register address) is automatically increased to allow multiple data read/writes.</q></p>
    <p>So if we want to read multiple registers successively in one transaction, starting at 0x28 (where the low byte of the x-acceleration is stored), we need to use <c>0x28 | (1&lt;&lt;7)</c> for the register address from which to start reading.</p>
    <note>≈ 1 min, her slide 17's shape: the addresses from the header, and the datasheet's auto-increment rule in its own words.  The transfer tables are the next slide.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-autoincrement-tables">
    <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/i2c_transfer_reads.svg" width="94%"/>
    <caption>Table 22 is yesterday's single-register read; Table 23 is today's: after each DATA byte the controller ACKs (MAK) and the chip serves the next register, until the final NACK (the tables' Master is our controller, their Slave our target).</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The gloss line under Table 23 is the auto-increment rule.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                <c>lsm303agr.h</c> declares a structure that holds one
                three-axis reading, and a pointer to a variable of this
                type is what <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c> fills in:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
typedef struct {
	int16_t x;
	int16_t y;
	int16_t z;
} lsm303AccelData_s;
]]></code></program>

            <!-- Byte assembly: its own beat (Gate 1 item 4) — the claim is
                 load-bearing for Lab 7 D1.  The listing below and
                 sl-day13x-readraw's copy are two copies of one function
                 (change them together); check_starters.py guards the code
                 against lsm303agr_partial.c. -->
            <p>
                The six bytes come back <em>low byte first</em>:
                <c>OUT_X_L_A</c> at 0x28 holds the low byte of x and
                <c>OUT_X_H_A</c> at 0x29 the high byte.
                <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c>, which we'll hand you, makes
                the six-byte read and then
                assembles each pair into one signed 16-bit value:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s *result) {
	uint8_t data[6];

	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, LSM303_OUT_X_L_A | (1<<7), 6, &data[0]);

	result->x = ((int16_t) data[1] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[0]);
	result->y = ((int16_t) data[3] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[2]);
	result->z = ((int16_t) data[5] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[4]);
}
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The line
                <c>result-&gt;x = ((int16_t) data[1] &lt;&lt; 8) | ((int16_t) data[0]);</c>
                widens each byte, moves the high byte up
                eight places, ORs them, and stores the result in an
                <c>int16_t</c>, which is where bit 15 becomes the sign.
                This is a
                different use of the shift and OR operators than we have
                seen so far: here they build a wider signed value out of
                two array elements, rather than setting a bit inside a
                register.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-readraw">
    <p><c>lsm303agr.h</c> declares a structure for one three-axis reading, and <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c>, given to you, fills it: one six-byte auto-increment read, then each pair of bytes is assembled low-byte-first (<c>data[0]</c> is <c>OUT_X_L_A</c>) into a signed 16-bit value.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
typedef struct {
	int16_t x;
	int16_t y;
	int16_t z;
} lsm303AccelData_s;

void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s *result) {
	uint8_t data[6];

	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, LSM303_OUT_X_L_A | (1<<7), 6, &data[0]);

	result->x = ((int16_t) data[1] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[0]);
	result->y = ((int16_t) data[3] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[2]);
	result->z = ((int16_t) data[5] << 8) | ((int16_t) data[4]);
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>This function uses i2c.c and lsm303agr.h: it is the device-driver layer from Day 10, now for the accelerometer.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  The listing is abridged for the wall (the file's comment blocks are off it); the full file arrives tomorrow as lsm303agr_partial.c.  Low byte first: data[0] is OUT_X_L_A.  The shift and OR build a wider signed value from two array elements — a new use of familiar operators, worth saying out loud.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 — her slide 11, deliberately re-ordered to the end
             (Gate 1): the control-register question is the last thing said
             before the homework that answers it.  NOT cuttable — it is the
             sole in-class setup for the homework Day 14's opening commit
             stands on. -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-control-registers">
            <title>Part 5: Settings for Basic Accelerometer Operation</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-31.  The table below and
                 sl-day13x-control-regs' table are two copies of one list
                 (the slide's copy projects; this one is the reading's):
                 change them together. -->
            <p>
                We now know how to read the data out of the sensor.  What
                we have not done yet is tell the sensor how to run.  The
                accelerometer has six control registers, all reachable
                over I2C.  Only two of them matter for us.  The rest are
                about features we leave at their defaults.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="86%" margins="7%">
            <tabular halign="left">
                <col width="30%"/><col width="70%"/>
                <row header="yes"><cell>Register</cell><cell>What it governs</cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG1_A</c></cell><cell>Data rate, power mode (the <c>LPen</c> bit, low-power enable), axis selection.  <em>Ours to set.</em></cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG2_A</c></cell><cell>High-pass filter configuration (§8.7); bypassed by default.</cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG3_A</c></cell><cell>Interrupt selection (none used; off by default).</cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG4_A</c></cell><cell>Full-scale range (±2, ±4, ±8, ±16 g) and the <c>HR</c> bit (high resolution); together with <c>LPen</c> in <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> it picks the resolution mode.  <em>Ours to set.</em></cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG5_A</c></cell><cell>Interrupt configuration (unused; defaults).</cell></row>
                <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG6_A</c></cell><cell>Interrupt configuration (unused; defaults).</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            </sidebyside>

            <p>
                Everything we did today assumed the ±2 g range; the
                sensitivity derivation used normal mode, and the worked
                conversions used low-power.
                <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> and <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c> are where both
                choices actually get made, and working out their settings
                from the datasheet is part of tonight's homework
                (<xref ref="sec-accel-day14-before"/>).
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day13x-control-regs">
    <p>The accelerometer has six control registers, all reachable over I2C.  Only two of them matter for us.  The rest are about features we leave at their defaults.</p>
    <tabular halign="left">
        <col width="30%"/><col width="70%"/>
        <row header="yes"><cell>Register</cell><cell>What it governs</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG1_A</c></cell><cell>Data rate, power mode (the <c>LPen</c> bit, low-power enable), axis selection.  <em>Ours to set.</em></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG2_A</c></cell><cell>High-pass filter configuration (§8.7); bypassed by default.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG3_A</c></cell><cell>Interrupt selection (none used; off by default).</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG4_A</c></cell><cell>Full-scale range (±2, ±4, ±8, ±16 g) and the <c>HR</c> bit (high resolution); together with <c>LPen</c> in <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> it picks the resolution mode.  <em>Ours to set.</em></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG5_A</c></cell><cell>Interrupt configuration (unused; defaults).</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>CTRL_REG6_A</c></cell><cell>Interrupt configuration (unused; defaults).</cell></row>
    </tabular>
    <note>≈ 3 min, her slide 11 moved to the end on purpose: this question is the last thing said before the homework that answers it.  Everything we did today assumed ±2 g — the derivation used normal mode and the worked conversions low-power; CTRL_REG1_A and CTRL_REG4_A are where both choices get made.  End-of-class beat: protected, do not cut.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Close -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day13x-homework">
            <title>Homework for Thursday</title>

            <!-- Prose WRITTEN 2026-08-31, matching Day 13 Part 8's list
                 and her slide 19. -->
