    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================
         Day 14 Before Class.  Week 7 session 4 (plans/day14-prompt.md)
         writes this section: the datasheet scavenger hunt (CTRL_REG1/4
         bit fields; the ANSWERS 0x77/0x00 stay out of the reading), plus
         the stationary-reading subsection in her decks' framing (Q8:
         flat ≈ +1000 mg, flipped ≈ −1000 mg; NO proper-acceleration/
         free-fall hook).  Citations: §8.6 Tables 33–35 (p. 47), §8.9
         Tables 41–42 (p. 49) of external/datasheets/lsm303agr.pdf.
         ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-accel-day14-before">
        <title>Before Class: The Datasheet Scavenger Hunt</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                On Thursday we'll complete the accelerometer's device driver
                and watch live acceleration data stream to CoolTerm.  To get
                ready for that class you have two pieces of homework: writing
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> on paper, and working out
                what settings belong in the two control registers,
                <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> and <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>.  This reading has
                two goals.  The first is to say what a working accelerometer
                reads when it is sitting still, which is how you'll know on
                Thursday whether your driver is right.  The second is a
                scavenger hunt through the datasheet, to get familiar with
                the many kinds of information in it; on the way you will
                pass the tables from which the two register settings come.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-stationary">
            <title>What a Stationary Accelerometer Reads</title>
            <p>
                Gravity is itself an acceleration, so an accelerometer that
                is not moving at all is still measuring something: each axis
                measures how much of gravity's 1 g lies along it, and the
                sensor's convention is that an axis pointing up reads
                positive.  The units of the
                sensor's output are g, where 1 g is the acceleration of
                gravity, 9.8 m/s², and one milli-g (mg) is a thousandth of
                that.  So with the board lying flat on the table, component
                side up, z reads approximately +1000 mg
                and x and y read approximately 0 mg.
            </p>
            <p>
                Flip the board over and the z reading changes sign, to
                approximately −1000 mg.  Tilt or rotate the board and
                gravity's 1000 mg moves between the three axes, and that is
                what we'll use on Thursday to measure tilt.  A
                <q>perfect</q> value is 1000, but a real board will be off by
                a few tens of milli-g, because the sensor has a zero-g
                offset: the datasheet says a flat, motionless sensor
                typically reads ±40 mg away from the true value, and its Min
                and Max columns allow ±80 mg.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-hunt-reg1">
            <title>CTRL_REG1_A: Data Rate, Power Mode, and Axes</title>
            <p>
                Open the
                <url href="external/datasheets/lsm303agr.pdf">LSM303AGR
                datasheet</url> to §8.6 (p. 47).  Tables 33 and 34 describe
                <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c>, at register address 0x20: which bit is
                which, and what each field selects
                (<xref ref="fig-ctrl-reg1-fields"/>).  The top four bits,
                ODR[3:0] (ODR here is the output data rate, a different ODR
                than the GPIO output data register from the earlier
                chapters), set how often the
                sensor takes a reading; the LPen bit (low-power enable)
                selects between normal mode and low-power mode; and the
                bottom three bits, Zen, Yen and Xen, enable the three axes
                independently, one bit per axis.
            </p>

            <!-- Reading figures: her Day 14 slide 6's own datasheet pulls,
                 used raw (they ARE the datasheet's tables; no shape-layer
                 annotations to lose).  Her "Setting: 0b..." callouts are
                 deliberately NOT carried into the reading (P-15): the
                 assembled bytes are Thursday's reveal.  Do not add them. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-ctrl-reg1-fields">
                <caption>Tables 33 and 34 of the LSM303AGR datasheet (§8.6,
                p. 47): the eight bits of <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> and what each
                field selects.  ODR[3:0] is the output data rate, LPen
                enables low-power mode, and Zen, Yen, and Xen enable the
                three axes.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/ctrl_reg1_fields.png" width="82%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The data rates themselves are in Table 35, on the same page
                (<xref ref="fig-ctrl-reg1-rates"/>): each nonzero ODR[3:0]
                pattern selects one rate, from 1 Hz up to the kilohertz
                range, and 0000 is power-down.  Here
                is what we want from the sensor this week: readings at
                400 Hz, in normal mode, with all three axes enabled.
                Find the four ODR bits that select 400 Hz, decide what LPen
                must be, and decide what the three axis-enable bits must be.
                Write the resulting eight-bit value down; that is your
                <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> setting, and on Thursday we'll compare
                answers before we put it into the sensor.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ctrl-reg1-rates">
                <caption>Table 35 of the LSM303AGR datasheet (§8.6, p. 47):
                the data rate selected by each ODR[3:0] pattern.  One of
                these rows is the 400 Hz we want.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/ctrl_reg1_rates.png" width="82%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-hunt-reg4">
            <title>CTRL_REG4_A: Range and Resolution</title>
            <p>
                The second register of the pair is <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>, at
                address 0x23, described in §8.9 (p. 49), Tables 41 and 42
                (<xref ref="fig-ctrl-reg4-fields"/>).  The two fields that
                matter for us are FS[1:0] (full scale), which selects the
                measurement range: ±2 g, ±4 g, ±8 g, or ±16 g, and HR (high
                resolution), which is the second half of the mode selection:
                LPen in <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> and HR here together pick one of
                the three resolution modes.  We want ±2 g, which is the
                range we assumed in every conversion we worked yesterday.
                The remaining fields, BDU (block data update), BLE
                (big/little endian data selection), the self-test bits, and
                SPI_ENABLE, which picks the 3-wire form of the SPI interface
                we are not using (the chip is on I2C because the breakout
                ties its chip-select pin high, not because of this bit), we
                leave at their default values, which Table 42
                lists.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ctrl-reg4-fields">
                <caption>Tables 41 and 42 of the LSM303AGR datasheet (§8.9,
                p. 49): the eight bits of <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>.  FS[1:0]
                selects the full-scale range, and HR is half of the
                resolution-mode selection; the other fields stay at their
                defaults.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/ctrl_reg4_fields.png" width="82%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                How LPen and HR combine is in a different part of the
                datasheet: §4.2.1, Table 14 (p. 27), reproduced in
                <xref ref="fig-accel-modes"/>.  Each mode row lists its data
                width and its sensitivity at ±2 g, and the last row is a
                disallowed combination.  We want normal
                mode, the 10-bit row.  Read off what LPen and HR must be for
                that row, then assemble your eight-bit <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>
                value the same way you assembled <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-modes">
                <caption>Table 14 of the LSM303AGR datasheet (§4.2.1, p. 27):
                the three operating modes, and one disallowed combination.
                The LPen bit of
                <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> and the HR bit of <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>
                together select the row, and each mode row lists its data
                width and its sensitivity at ±2 g in mg/digit.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/accel_modes.png" width="82%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-hunt-tour">
            <title>The Rest of the Hunt</title>
            <p>
                A datasheet is not read cover to cover; the skill is jumping
                to the part that answers your question, and that takes
                knowing roughly what is in the document.  So before you
                close the PDF, make four more stops.  Each one is a place
                this course has already leaned on, or is about to.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Go to §2.1, Table 3 (pp. 13–14), the sensor
                characteristics.  Find the zero-g offset row and read off
                its typical value; this is the number behind the
                <q>a few tens of milli-g off</q> in
                <xref ref="subsec-day14-stationary"/>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Go to §7, Table 26 (p. 43), the register address
                map.  Find the six output registers, <c>OUT_X_L_A</c>
                through <c>OUT_Z_H_A</c>, and note their addresses; these
                are the registers <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c> reads in one
                auto-increment transfer.  Notice the map also lists the
                magnetometer's registers and a temperature-sensor
                configuration register; we use neither.</p></li>
                <li><p>Go to §8.4 (p. 46) and find WHO_AM_I_A, the register
                Tuesday's test program read; the fixed value 0x33 is
                printed there as its default.</p></li>
                <li><p>Go to §6.1.1, Tables 20–23 (p. 38), the four I2C
                transfer diagrams.  Table 22 is the read you captured on
                Tuesday, and Table 20 is the write you will make on
                Thursday.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-accel-day14">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-day14-stationary">
                <statement><p>Your board lies flat and motionless on the
                table, component side up.  Roughly what should the three
                converted readings be, in milli-g?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Approximately 0 on x, 0 on y, and
                        +1000 on z.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Each axis measures how much
                        of gravity's 1 g lies along it, and with the board
                        flat, all of it lands on z, whose upward direction
                        reads positive.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Approximately 0 on all three axes,
                        because the board is not moving.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Gravity is an acceleration, and each
                        axis measures how much of it lies along that axis.
                        Flat on the table, component side up, z reads
                        approximately +1000 mg.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Approximately +1000 on every
                        axis.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Each axis measures only the part of
                        gravity that lies along that axis.  Flat on the
                        table, gravity lies along z alone, so x and y read
                        near zero.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Approximately −1000 on z.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The sign is worth getting right: −1000 mg
                        on z is what the board reads <em>flipped over</em>,
                        with z pointing down.  Component side up, z points up
                        and reads approximately +1000 mg.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-day14-lpen">
                <statement><p>According to Table 34, what does writing 1 to
                the LPen bit of <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> select?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Low-power mode.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  LPen is the low-power enable:
                        0 is normal mode and 1 is low-power mode, and
                        Table 14 shows what that choice does to the data
                        width.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A lower output data rate.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The data rate has its own field, the
                        four ODR bits.  LPen selects the power mode, which
                        sets the data width, not the rate.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>High-resolution mode.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The HR bit that selects high-resolution
                        mode is in <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>, not here.  LPen = 1
                        selects low-power mode, the 8-bit
                        row of Table 14.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It powers the sensor down.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Power-down is an ODR setting: ODR[3:0]
                        = 0000 in Table 35.  LPen = 1 keeps the sensor
                        running, in low-power (8-bit) mode.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-day14-fs">
                <statement><p>According to Table 42, which FS[1:0] pattern in
                <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c> selects the ±2 g full-scale
                range?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>00</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  00 is ±2 g, and it is also the
                        register's default.  The other patterns select the
                        wider ranges, at the cost of coarser
                        steps.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>01</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>01 selects ±4 g.  The table's order runs
                        ±2, ±4, ±8, ±16 g as the pattern counts up; ±2 g is
                        00.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>10</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>10 selects ±8 g.  The ±2 g range we want
                        is pattern 00, the smallest range and the finest
                        steps.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>11</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>11 selects ±16 g, the widest range and
                        the coarsest steps.  The ±2 g range is
                        00.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-day14-modes">
                <statement><p>According to Table 14, with LPen = 0 and
                HR = 0 the accelerometer is in which operating
                mode?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Normal mode, with 10-bit data
                        output.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  That is the middle row of
                        Table 14, and it is the mode we'll run this week: 10
                        valid bits, 4 mg per digit at ±2 g.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Low-power mode, with 8-bit data
                        output.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Low-power mode is the LPen = 1, HR = 0
                        row.  With both bits 0 the sensor is in normal mode,
                        10 bits.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>High-resolution mode, with 12-bit data
                        output.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>High-resolution mode needs HR = 1.  With
                        both bits 0 the sensor is in normal mode, 10
                        bits.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The combination is not
                        allowed.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The disallowed combination is LPen = 1
                        with HR = 1, the table's last row.  Both bits 0 is
                        normal mode.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-day14-offset">
                <statement><p>According to Table 3 of the LSM303AGR
                datasheet, what is the typical zero-g offset of an
                accelerometer axis?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>±40 mg</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  That is the typical value;
                        the table's Min and Max columns allow ±80 mg.  It
                        is why a flat board reads close to, but rarely
                        exactly, +1000 mg on z.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>±80 mg</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>±80 mg is the table's Min and Max
                        bound, the worst case.  The typical column says
                        ±40 mg.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>±4 mg</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>4 mg is the sensitivity per digit in
                        normal mode at ±2 g, a different row of the same
                        table.  The typical zero-g offset is
                        ±40 mg.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>0 mg; a stationary axis reads
                        exactly its share of gravity.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A real sensor carries a small constant
                        offset; the datasheet says ±40 mg is typical.  That
                        is why the reading is <q>approximately</q>
                        +1000 mg, not exactly.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-day14-outaddr">
                <statement><p>According to Table 26 of the LSM303AGR
                datasheet, at what address is <c>OUT_X_L_A</c>, the low
                byte of the x-axis reading?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>0x28</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The six output registers run
                        from 0x28 to 0x2D, and the auto-increment read
                        starts there.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>0x20</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>0x20 is <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c>, the first
                        control register.  The output registers start at
                        0x28.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>0x0F</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>0x0F is WHO_AM_I_A.  The output
                        registers start at 0x28.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>0x32</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>0x32 is the sensor's 8-bit I2C
                        address, not a register address inside it.  The
                        output registers start at 0x28.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

