    <section xml:id="sec-accel-day14">
        <title>Day 14 In-Class: Finish the Driver, See the Data, Measure Tilt</title>

        <introduction>
            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose polish only. The introduction is written
                 (it frames the day); do not add teaching to it. -->
            <p>
                You worked out the two control-register settings on paper,
                and you drafted <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c>.  Today
                we'll put both to work: complete the device driver, watch
                live x-y-z data stream to CoolTerm, plot it, and work out how
                tilt follows from what the sensor measures.  By the end of
                class your own driver will be reading the accelerometer,
                which is the pre-lab for Lab 7.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 2 (Part 1 was the cut HiTA slot; numbering kept so
             plans/week7.md cross-references stay valid) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-settings">
            <title>Part 2: The Settings, Committed and Revealed</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: connecting prose.  OWES, by id: a lead
                 paragraph tying the reading's guided walk to the commit
                 (act-day14-settings), and after the reveal one paragraph
                 landing 0x77/0x00 in running text with the
                 rounds-3.9-to-4 clause (ins-day14-settings carries it
                 meanwhile, instructor-only).  The sixty-second-look
                 sentence below is written now because the deck's first
                 slides assert it. -->
            <p>
                Today's driver needs one byte in <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> and one
                in <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>, and we'll settle both now.  We'll
                look once more at the four table images from the reading:
                the fields of <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c>
                (<xref ref="fig-ctrl-reg1-fields"/>), the data rates
                (<xref ref="fig-ctrl-reg1-rates"/>), the fields of
                <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c> (<xref ref="fig-ctrl-reg4-fields"/>), and
                the operating modes (<xref ref="fig-accel-modes"/>).  We
                want readings at 400 Hz, in normal mode, on all three axes,
                over the ±2 g range, and everything needed to build the two
                bytes is in those four tables.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-day14-settings">
                <title>Compare the two settings at your table</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>We want readings at 400 Hz, in normal mode, with all
                    three axes enabled, over the ±2 g range.  You worked
                    the two bytes out for homework, and they go into your
                    driver later today.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-settings-reg1">
                    <statement><p>Compare your <c>CTRL_REG1_A</c> byte with
                    your table group, field by field: the four ODR bits,
                    LPen, then Zen, Yen, and Xen.  Settle any disagreement
                    against the datasheet's tables.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-settings-reg4">
                    <statement><p>Do the same for <c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>, and
                    agree on which operating mode your LPen and HR choices
                    select in Table 14 of the LSM303AGR
                    datasheet.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day14-settings">
                <p><c>CTRL_REG1_A</c>, field by field: ODR[3:0] = 0111 is
                the 400 Hz row of Table 35 of the LSM303AGR datasheet;
                LPen = 0 keeps normal mode; and
                Zen = Yen = Xen = 1 enables all three axes.  Assembled:
                <c>0b01110111</c> = 0x77.</p>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/ctrl_bytes_filled.svg" width="92%"/>
                <p><c>CTRL_REG4_A</c>: BDU = 0 (continuous update), BLE = 0,
                FS[1:0] = 00 selects ±2 g, HR = 0, and the self-test and SPI
                bits stay 0.  Assembled: <c>0b00000000</c> = 0x00.  With
                LPen = 0 and HR = 0, Table 14 says we are in normal mode:
                10-bit readings.  Its sensitivity column prints 4 mg/digit
                at ±2 g; the 3.9 mg/digit we derived yesterday is the same
                number, before the table rounds it.</p>
            </instructor>

            <!-- Her slide 6, at export resolution: all four tables on one
                 slide with the two Setting callouts and the color-coded
                 boxes (her comment of 2026-09-01 asked for exactly this
                 as a second instructor slide; she supplied the file). -->
