        <!-- Part 4 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-driver">
            <title>Part 4: Complete the Driver</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: connecting prose.  OWES, by id: a lead
                 paragraph walking what lsm303agr_partial.c gives and what
                 is blank (the listing below asserts it; the walk narrates
                 it) — BULLETIZE the four-functions/three-states inventory
                 to match sl-day14-partial's four bullets rather than one
                 compound sentence (Gate 2' load 5) — and a closing
                 paragraph on what the uint8_t return
                 reports and how accel_test.c uses it (currently carried by
                 the paragraph before ins-day14-accelinit).  The listing is
                 guarded by check_starters.py (lsm303_AccelInit fragment
                 pair). -->
            <p>
                <c>lsm303agr_partial.c</c> is most of a driver.  Of the four
                functions the header declares,
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c> and
                <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c> are given whole:
                RegisterRead is the function that was included in
                <c>whoami_test.c</c>, and we went through ReadRaw
                yesterday.  <c>lsm303_AccelInit()</c> is given except for
                the two register values, which you will fill in.
                Only <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> is entirely yours to
                write:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/**************************************************************************
 * Write one byte to an accelerometer register (Datasheet, Table 20)
 */
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data) {
	// complete this function


}
]]></code></program>

            <!-- The model beside the blank: the same function whoami_test.c
                 carried (its code is guarded by that file's whole-file pair
                 in check_starters.py).  This copy matches
                 lsm303agr_partial.c verbatim: the file's stale "Table 13"
                 citation was corrected to Table 22 with her approval
                 (2026-09-01). -->
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/**************************************************************************
 * Read one byte from an accelerometer register (Datasheet, Table 22)
 */
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

	return data;
}
]]></code></program>

            <activity xml:id="act-day14-registerwrite">
                <title>Write the register-write function</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-registerwrite">
                    <statement><p>Complete
                    <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c>, on your own,
                    from your paper draft,
                    with the given <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c> as the
                    model.  It takes one I2C library call.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-partial">
    <ul>
        <li><c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c> and <c>lsm303_AccelReadRaw()</c> are given whole.  RegisterRead is the function that was included in <c>whoami_test.c</c>, and we went through ReadRaw yesterday.</li>
        <li><c>lsm303_AccelInit()</c> is given except for the two register values, which you will fill in.</li>
        <li><c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> is entirely yours to write.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  Orientation only; the file itself goes on screen next.  The split is her design: the homework drafted RegisterWrite on paper, and the reading derived the two AccelInit values.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-attempt">
    <p>Complete <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c>, on your own, from your paper draft, with the given read function as the model.  It takes one I2C library call.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data) {
	// complete this function


}
]]></code></program>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* The model: the given read function */
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress) {
	uint8_t data;
	i2c1_memRead(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);

	return data;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 2 min, on their own.  This was the homework; do not offer an out (her ruling, 2026-09-01).  A student with the paper draft transcribes it.  Then the reveal.</note>
</slide>

            <!-- Her comments of 2026-09-01 shaped this beat: explain the
                 read-vs-write difference through Tuesday's WHO_AM_I read,
                 and show read-one-byte beside write-one-byte (Table 21,
                 which we never use, is off the wall).  The composite
                 i2c_transfer_read_vs_write.svg reuses the shared base and
                 glosses; the Day 13 SVGs are untouched. -->
            <p>
                On Tuesday we read from one of the accelerometer's
                registers: the controller sent the accelerometer's address,
                then the register's sub-address, and the accelerometer sent
                the register's contents back
                (<xref ref="fig-i2c-read-vs-write"/>, Table 22).  Writing
                to a register begins the same way: the address, then the
                sub-address, and then, instead of reading a byte back, we
                send the value we want written (Table 20).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-read-vs-write">
                <caption>Tuesday's read beside today's write, in the
                datasheet's own tables (§6.1.1, p. 38).  Table 22 is the
                single-register read that <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterRead()</c>
                makes; Table 20 is the single-register write that
                <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> makes: the same device
                address and sub-address, and a data byte sent in place of
                the read-back.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/i2c_transfer_read_vs_write.svg" width="92%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-write-transfer">
    <p>On Tuesday we read from one of the accelerometer's registers: the controller sent the accelerometer's address, then the register's sub-address, and the accelerometer sent the register's contents back (Table 22).  Writing to a register begins the same way: the address, then the sub-address, and then, instead of reading a byte back, we send the value we want written (Table 20).</p>
    <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/i2c_transfer_read_vs_write.svg" width="94%"/>
    <p>In Table 20's row, <c>LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL</c> is the SAD + W cell, <c>RegisterAddress</c> is the SUB cell, the count of 1 is the single DATA cell, and <c>&amp;data</c> is what that cell carries.</p>
    <note>≈ 2 min, after the reveal.  Do not re-teach the rows; the point is read-versus-write, then the argument-to-cell mapping.</note>
</slide>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day14-registerwrite">
                <p>One call does the whole job:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data) {
	i2c1_memWrite(LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL, RegisterAddress, 1, &data);
}
]]></code></program>
                <p><c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> wants a pointer to the data and a
                byte count, which is why the argument is <c>&amp;data</c>
                and the count is 1.  This is also why the write uses
                <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> and not <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c>: the
                transaction sends two pieces of data after the device
                address, the register address and the value, and
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> has no register address argument to
                carry the first of them.</p>
            </instructor>

            <!-- The AccelInit skeleton, as the file ships it: the blanks
                 stay blank here (P-15 — the values were committed in
                 Part 2 and land in ins-day14-accelinit).  Guarded by
                 check_starters.py. -->
            <p>
                <c>lsm303_AccelInit()</c> needs no new I2C work, only your
                two values in its blanks:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
uint8_t lsm303_AccelInit() {
	// Check the WHOAMI register
	if (lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A) != 0x33) {
		return 0;		// failure
	}
	else {
		/******* Control register 1 (Datasheet 8.6) *******/
		uint8_t ctrl_reg1 =      ; // normal power mode @ 400 Hz, all axes enabled
		lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A, ctrl_reg1);

		/******* Control register 4 (Datasheet 8.9) *******/
		uint8_t ctrl_reg4 =      ; // continuous update, 2g full scale, I2C
		lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A, ctrl_reg4);

		return 1;		// success
	}
}
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The function begins with the WHO_AM_I check from Tuesday and
                returns 0 if the expected 0x33 does not come back, and 1 if
                initialization succeeded.  That <c>uint8_t</c> return value
                is for the caller: a test program can ask whether the sensor
                is actually there before trusting any readings, and in a
                moment you will see <c>accel_test.c</c> use that
                answer.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-day14-accelinit">
                <title>Fill in the two settings</title>
                <statement><p>Put your two values into the
                blanks in <c>lsm303_AccelInit()</c> and build.  Fix
                any compile errors, but do not run anything yet; the test
                program comes next.</p></statement>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day14-accelinit">
                <p>Here are the two lines, filled in.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
		uint8_t ctrl_reg1 = 0b01110111; // normal power mode @ 400 Hz, all axes enabled

		uint8_t ctrl_reg4 = 0b00000000; // continuous update, 2g full scale, I2C
]]></code></program>
                <p>Hex works just as well (0x77 and 0x00); the binary form
                shows the fields.</p>
            </instructor>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-accelinit">
    <p><c>lsm303_AccelInit()</c> needs no new I2C work, only your two values in its blanks:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
uint8_t lsm303_AccelInit() {
	// Check the WHOAMI register
	if (lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A) != 0x33) {
		return 0;		// failure
	}
	else {
		uint8_t ctrl_reg1 =      ; // normal power mode @ 400 Hz, all axes enabled
		lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A, ctrl_reg1);

		uint8_t ctrl_reg4 =      ; // continuous update, 2g full scale, I2C
		lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A, ctrl_reg4);

		return 1;		// success
	}
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The WHO_AM_I check and the uint8_t return are given: 0 reports failure, 1 success, and the test program is about to use that answer.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min.  The listing is abridged for the wall (the file's comment blocks are off it); the blanks stay blank here on purpose — the values come from the homework discussion, and the fill is the activity.  Point at the intent comments beside each blank: they are her wording for what the byte means.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5 — the CRUCIAL step -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-run">
            <title>Part 5: Run It</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: connecting prose.  OWES, by id: a lead
                 paragraph for the read-before-you-run beat (the walk of
                 accel_test.c below), and a short paragraph tying the tests
                 to the reading's stationary-reading expectation
                 (subsec-day14-stationary by xref).  The scaling-line and
                 paired-print explanations are written now because the
                 listing asserts both and the diagnostic ladder leans on
                 them.  The listing is guarded by check_starters.py
                 (accel_test.c whole-file pair). -->
            <p>
                Before you run <c>accel_test.c</c>, read it.  It is the
                driver's unit test, and every line it prints tells you
                something you will need when you are looking for a fault:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* Shell program to test LSM303AGR driver code
 *
 * ENGS 28
 * Description:	 	Simple calls to the driver functions for my LSM303AGR accelerometer,
 * 					to test basic functionality.
 *
 * Target device:	Nucleo C031C6, Adafruit LSM303AGR breakout
 * Dependencies:	lsm303agr, i2c.c, uart.c
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#include "lsm303agr.h"
#include "i2c.h"

#define ACC_FS 4
#define ACC_REGISTERWIDTH 16
#define MILLI 1000

/* CODE */
int main(void) {
  	// Setup code (run once) goes here:
	int16_t accel_x, accel_y, accel_z;
	lsm303AccelData_s accel_raw;

	i2c1_init();
	uart2_init();
	printf("LSM303AGR test program \n\r");

	if( lsm303_AccelInit() ) {
		printf("Accelerometer initialized! \n\r");
	}
	else {
		printf("Could not connect to accelerometer \n\r");
	}

  	printf("Control Register 1 = %x \n\r", lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A));
  	printf("Control Register 4 = %x \n\r", lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A));

	while(1) {
		lsm303_AccelReadRaw(&accel_raw);
		accel_x = (ACC_FS * accel_raw.x * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;
		accel_y = (ACC_FS * accel_raw.y * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;
		accel_z = (ACC_FS * accel_raw.z * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;

		printf("accel_raw_x=%x, accel_raw_y=%x, accel_raw_z=%x \n\r", accel_raw.x, accel_raw.y, accel_raw.z);
		printf("accel_x=%d mg, accel_y=%d mg, accel_z=%d mg \n\n\r", accel_x, accel_y, accel_z);

		delay_ms(2000);
	}

	return 0;		/* never reached */
}
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The program checks
                <c>lsm303_AccelInit()</c>'s return value and prints which way
                it went.  It also reads both control registers back and
                prints them, so you can check that the values you worked
                out were actually written.  The scaling line is
                yesterday's formula in C, with the <c>#define</c>s from
                yesterday and a right shift by 16 where the formula said
                divide by 2¹⁶ (the arithmetic behind the shift, including
                what happens to the large intermediate product, is in
                <xref ref="subsec-accel-ref-signed-shift"/>).  Each pass
                through the loop prints the raw readings
                in hex and the converted milli-g values right underneath.
                <xref ref="fig-coolterm-plainview"/> shows what a
                successful run looks like.
            </p>

            <!-- PLACEHOLDER (Petra, 2026-09-01): she will capture the
                 real screenshot when the accelerometer is set up again.
                 Drop path: assets/images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/
                 coolterm_plainview.png — then only the <image source>
                 line below changes.  REMIND HER at the next delivery. -->
            <figure xml:id="fig-coolterm-plainview">
                <caption>CoolTerm's output from a successful run of
                <c>accel_test.c</c>: the opening line, the initialization
                message, the two control-register readbacks, and then a
                raw and converted line pair every two seconds.
                (Placeholder; the real screenshot is coming.)</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/coolterm_plainview_placeholder.svg" width="82%"/>
            </figure>

            <activity xml:id="act-day14-run">
                <title>Stream your first acceleration data</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-run-build">
                    <statement><p>Build, run, and open CoolTerm.  You should
                    see the <c>LSM303AGR test program</c> line,
                    <c>Accelerometer initialized!</c>, the
                    two control-register readbacks, and then a new reading
                    every two seconds.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-run-tests">
                    <statement><p>Test it against gravity: flat on the
                    table, z should read approximately +1000 mg.  Flip the
                    board over and the sign flips.  Rotate the board to move
                    gravity onto x and then y.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-run-raw">
                    <statement><p>While the board lies flat, pick one
                    printed line and check the z pair: does
                    the raw hex value convert to the milli-g value printed
                    beside it, by yesterday's formula?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day14-run-line">
                <p>One line, worked.  Flat on the table, the raw
                z reading is about 0x4000 = 16384, and
                (4 × 16384 × 1000) ≫ 16 = 1000 mg.  A real board is a few
                tens of milli-g off, per the zero-g offset.</p>
            </instructor>

            <!-- The diagnostic ladder is written now (the ladder slide
                 asserts it).  Checkpoint mechanics are deck presenter
                 notes only (S-25).  Rung 1 deliberately does NOT assert a
                 causal verdict for the print (Q3, Day 13 gate item 23). -->
            <p>
                When the program runs and the output is wrong, the printout
                narrows it to one layer.  If
                <c>Could not connect to accelerometer</c>
                appears, the WHO_AM_I check did not return 0x33:
                double-check your wiring against
                <xref ref="fig-accel-bb"/> and try
                again.  If the connection succeeds but
                a control-register readback is not what you wrote, the write
                path is suspect, and that is today's new code: check that
                your <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> makes a single
                <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> call, with the device address, the
                register address, a count of 1, and a pointer to the data
                byte.  If everything prints and both readbacks match, but
                the values are nowhere near 1000 mg on any axis at rest,
                the readings are arriving, and the values with which you
                initialized the control registers are
                the suspect: the scaling in <c>accel_test.c</c> assumes
                ±2 g, so a different FS[1:0] scales every axis by the ratio
                of the ranges.  Recompute one printed line by hand against
                yesterday's formula, then compare those two values against
                the readbacks on the
                screen.  If none of those three finds it, put the logic
                analyzer back on the bus as you did on Tuesday and watch
                the transaction itself.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-accel-test-setup">
    <p>Before you run <c>accel_test.c</c>, read it.  Here is the setup half.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
int main(void) {
	lsm303AccelData_s accel_raw;

	i2c1_init();
	uart2_init();
	printf("LSM303AGR test program \n\r");

	if( lsm303_AccelInit() ) {
		printf("Accelerometer initialized! \n\r");
	}
	else {
		printf("Could not connect to accelerometer \n\r");
	}

  	printf("Control Register 1 = %x \n\r", lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A));
  	printf("Control Register 4 = %x \n\r", lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A));
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The first line of output shows whether the initialization succeeded, and the Control Register 1 readback lets you check that the value you worked out was actually written.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min.  Abridged for the wall: the includes, the header comment, the #define block (yesterday's ACC_FS/ACC_REGISTERWIDTH/MILLI, verbatim) and the accel_x/y/z declaration line are off it; the full file is in the book and in the download.  Worth saying out loud: CTRL_REG1's power-on default is 0x07, so a readback of 0x77 must have come from the student's own write; CTRL_REG4's default equals the intended 0x00, so that readback alone proves nothing.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-accel-test-loop">
    <p>The loop half reads the sensor, scales the readings, and prints them.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
	while(1) {
		lsm303_AccelReadRaw(&accel_raw);
		accel_x = (ACC_FS * accel_raw.x * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;
		accel_y = (ACC_FS * accel_raw.y * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;
		accel_z = (ACC_FS * accel_raw.z * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;

		printf("accel_raw_x=%x, accel_raw_y=%x, accel_raw_z=%x \n\r", accel_raw.x, accel_raw.y, accel_raw.z);
		printf("accel_x=%d mg, accel_y=%d mg, accel_z=%d mg \n\n\r", accel_x, accel_y, accel_z);

		delay_ms(2000);
	}
]]></code></program>
    <p>The scaling line is yesterday's formula in C, with a right shift by 16 where the formula said divide by 2¹⁶.  Each pass prints the raw readings in hex and the converted milli-g values right underneath.</p>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  The variables are int16_t; the big intermediate product (±131 million at most) exists only inside the arithmetic, which C carries out in a wider integer type.  If that question comes up, the Reference section has the full story, including the signed-shift subtlety.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-ladder">
    <ul>
        <li>If <c>Could not connect to accelerometer</c> appears, the WHO_AM_I check did not return 0x33.  Double-check your wiring against Tuesday's diagram and try again.</li>
        <li>If it connects but a control-register readback is not what you wrote, the write path is suspect, and that is today's new code.  Check that your <c>lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite()</c> makes a single <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> call, with the device address, the register address, a count of 1, and a pointer to the data byte.</li>
        <li>If everything prints and both readbacks match, but the values are nowhere near 1000 mg at rest, the values with which you initialized the control registers are the suspect.  Recompute one printed line by hand against yesterday's formula, then compare those two values against the readbacks.</li>
        <li>If none of those three finds it, put the logic analyzer back on the bus as you did on Tuesday and watch the transaction itself.</li>
        <li>Still stuck?  We're here to help!</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 4 min of worktime with this on the wall.  Checkpoint is the end of this Part, minute 58: a student not yet streaming gets the completed driver file, ONE-TO-ONE and not announced, and still runs their own board through the flip and rotate tests — what they did not finish is the write logic, whose reveal already graded their paper version.  A second re-seat failure is not software and the file will not fix it: priority triage now, not at the bell.  A board that fails triage twice is not a software problem: put that student on a working neighbor's stream so they can verify their own driver logic, and handle the hardware after class.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-library">
            <title>Part 6: Make It a Library</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: connecting prose.  OWES, by id: one lead
                 paragraph naming this as Day 10's promotion discipline on a
                 second device (act-day14-promote's tasks carry the steps
                 meanwhile). -->
            <activity xml:id="act-day14-promote">
                <title>Promote the driver to your library</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Once your driver works, you can move it out of your
                    project's <c>Src</c> folder into the <c>mylib</c>
                    folder, so that it becomes accessible to other projects
                    as well.  The header, <c>lsm303agr.h</c>, is already in
                    <c>mylib</c>; you put it there on Tuesday.  Follow
                    these steps:</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-rename">
                    <statement><p>Rename <c>lsm303agr_partial.c</c> to
                    <c>lsm303agr.c</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-move">
                    <statement><p>Copy <c>lsm303agr.c</c> into your
                    <c>mylib</c> folder, then delete it from the
                    <c>Accelerometer</c> project's <c>Src</c>
                    folder.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-rebuild">
                    <statement><p>Rebuild the project and run it again, to
                    confirm everything still works from its new
                    home.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 7 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-plot">
            <title>Part 7: Plot It</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: connecting prose.  OWES, by id: a lead
                 paragraph motivating the plot (numbers scrolling by are
                 hard to read as motion; a chart shows the shape), tied to
                 fig-coolterm-chartview.  The modification list and the
                 chart-view path are written now because the slides assert
                 them (Q7: menu wording verified against the current
                 CoolTerm build, and her screenshot). -->
            <p>
                A few changes turn <c>accel_test.c</c> into a data
                source CoolTerm can plot: comment out all the
                <c>printf()</c> statements, print the three values as one
                comma-separated line inside the <c>while(1)</c>
                loop instead, and shorten the delay so the plot moves.
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
printf("%d,%d,%d\n\r", accel_x, accel_y, accel_z);
delay_ms(100);      // or less
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                In CoolTerm, connect as usual.  Then, in the
                <em>View</em> menu, select <em>View Chart</em>
                (<xref ref="fig-coolterm-chartview"/>).  Each
                comma-separated value becomes one trace.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-plot-mod">
    <p>A few changes turn <c>accel_test.c</c> into a data source CoolTerm can plot.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Comment out all the <c>printf()</c> statements.</li>
        <li>Inside the <c>while(1)</c> loop, print the three values as one comma-separated line instead, and shorten the delay so the plot moves:</li>
    </ul>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
printf("%d,%d,%d\n\r", accel_x, accel_y, accel_z);
delay_ms(100);      // or less
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  Her slide 13's modification list.  The comma-separated line is what the chart parses: one value per trace.</note>
</slide>

