            <instructor xml:id="ins-day14-experiments">
                <p>What the traces do.  Flipping the board inverts z's
                sign.  Raising and lowering it periodically puts excursions
                above and below 1000 mg on z, at the rhythm of the motion.
                Banging the table puts one wide spike on all three traces at
                once, often railing at ±2000 mg, and it decays within a few
                samples.</p>
            </instructor>

            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-plotter">
                <caption>Three axes of acceleration in CoolTerm's chart
                view.  The steady levels are gravity's projection on each
                axis; the steps are the board being turned; the burst about
                two-thirds of the way across is shaking, and the flat
                traces after it are the board back at rest.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/coolterm_plotter.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-plotter" ref="fig-accel-plotter">
    <caption>Trace 1 is x, Trace 2 is y, Trace 3 is z: the steady levels are gravity; the steps are the board turning; the burst is shaking.</caption>
    <note>After the experiments, her slide 15; the reveal's minute is read over this slide.  Use it to name what the room just saw, not to replace seeing it.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 8 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-tilt">
            <title>Part 8: Tilt</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: connecting prose.  OWES, by id: a lead
                 paragraph posing the day's last question (from streaming
                 data to a tilt angle) ahead of act-day14-tilt-commit, and
                 a closing clause landing that ax and ay are the milli-g
                 numbers already on the screen (the angle comes out the
                 same from the raw values, since the scale factor cancels
                 in the ratio).  The geometry paragraphs are written now
                 because the two figures assert their formulas. -->
            <activity xml:id="act-day14-tilt-commit">
                <title>Predict the readings at 30 degrees</title>
                <statement><p>The board lies flat, then tilts by 30
                degrees about one edge.  Write down what happens to each
                of the three readings: which move, which direction, and
                roughly where they end up.</p></statement>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day14-tilt-commit">
                <p>Gravity's 1000 mg redistributes.  The z reading falls
                from 1000 to 1000 cos 30° ≈ 866 mg.  The in-plane axis
                whose positive direction now points up the slope reads
                +1000 sin 30° = 500 mg, and if you tip the breakout the
                other way, so that the positive axis points down the slope,
                that same axis reads −500 mg: a positive reading means that
                positive axis is above the horizon (AN-1057, p. 7).  The
                third axis stays near zero.  Nothing about the total
                changes: the vector still has length 1000 mg; it just
                projects differently.  This prediction is made in the flat
                frame, with z along gravity at rest; the two-axis formula
                later in this Part assumes a different frame, with gravity
                in the x-y plane instead.</p>
            </instructor>

            <p>
                This is inclination sensing, and Analog Devices wrote the
                application note the field uses:
                <url href="external/datasheets/an-1057.pdf">AN-1057,
                <em>Using an Accelerometer for Inclination Sensing</em></url>.
                From the acceleration data along one axis that is tilted
                (<xref ref="fig-tilt-single"/>), the x reading
                is <m>A_x = g \sin\theta</m>, so
                <m>\theta = \sin^{-1}(A_x/g)</m>: one axis is enough to
                derive the tilt angle, for small tilts.  That works, but not
                everywhere.  The slope of <m>\sin\theta</m> is
                <m>\cos\theta</m>, which is close to 1 near 0° and
                goes to zero as <m>\theta</m>
                approaches 90°, and this slope is the measurement's
                <term>sensitivity</term>.  Near 0° a 1° tilt moves the
                reading by
                about 17 mg, and near 90° the same 1° tilt moves it by less
                than 1 mg, so a fixed amount of sensor noise barely
                disturbs the angle near 0° but can swing the computed angle
                by tens of degrees near 90°.  And a single axis cannot
                distinguish all four quadrants, so there is no full-circle
                answer.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tilt-single">
                <caption>Single-axis tilt, from AN-1057 (Figure 1).  The x
                reading is the projection of gravity:
                <m>A_x = g \sin\theta</m>, so
                <m>\theta = \sin^{-1}(A_x/g)</m>.  The sine's slope
                flattens toward 90°, so the angle computed this way loses
                sensitivity there.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/tilt_single_axis.svg" width="88%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-tilt-single" ref="fig-tilt-single" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>One axis is enough to derive the tilt angle from small tilts: from the acceleration data along the one axis that was tilted, A_x = g sin θ, so θ = sin⁻¹(A_x / g).</li>
        <li><term>Sensitivity.</term> The slope of sin θ is cos θ: close to 1 near 0°, and it goes to zero at 90°.</li>
        <li>Near 0° a 1° tilt moves the reading by about 17 mg; near 90° the same 1° tilt moves it by less than 1 mg.</li>
        <li>So a fixed amount of sensor noise can swing the computed angle by tens of degrees near 90°.</li>
        <li>One axis also cannot tell all four quadrants apart.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 4 min.  The mechanism, not just the verdict (Gate 1): say "the slope goes to zero" while pointing at the tilted drawing.  AN-1057 is hosted with the course documents; Lab 7's optional full-orientation route is in it.</note>
</slide>

            <p>
                Two axes fix both problems
                (<xref ref="fig-tilt-two"/>).  The two-axis formula assumes
                gravity lies in the plane of the two axes you use, the
                geometry AN-1057's own Figure 7 draws, with y vertical and
                reading about +1000 mg at rest.  With
                <m>A_x = g \sin\theta</m> and <m>A_y = g \cos\theta</m>,
                the ratio gives <m>\theta = \tan^{-1}(A_x/A_y)</m>: the
                gravity magnitude cancels, the sensitivity is constant at
                every angle, and the signs of the two readings identify the
                quadrant, covering the full 360°.  C's math library handles
                the quadrants for you: <c>atan2(ax, ay)</c> takes
                the two readings separately and returns the angle in the
                correct quadrant, and the reason it needs both is in
                <xref ref="subsec-accel-ref-orientation"/>, which also
                gives the three-axis equations for a full orientation.
                Lab 7 asks you to watch the axis directions marked on your
                own breakout board, since which axis reads which sign
                depends on how the board sits.  Here <c>ax</c> and
                <c>ay</c> are the milli-g x and y
                readings already on your screen, and the angle comes out
                the same from the raw values, because the scale factor
                cancels in the ratio.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tilt-two">
                <caption>Two-axis tilt, from AN-1057 (Figure 7).  The ratio
                of the two projections gives
                <m>\theta = \tan^{-1}(A_x/A_y)</m>, with constant
                sensitivity and, if the quadrants are handled, the full
                360°.  C's <c>atan2()</c> handles the quadrants.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day14-Accelerometer(2)/tilt_two_axis.svg" width="88%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-tilt-two" ref="fig-tilt-two" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Two axes fix both problems.  With A_x = g sin θ and A_y = g cos θ, the ratio gives θ = tan⁻¹(A_x / A_y): the g cancels, the sensitivity is constant at every angle, and the signs of the two readings identify the quadrant, covering the full 360°.</li>
        <li>C's math library handles the quadrants for you: <c>atan2(ax, ay)</c> takes the two readings separately and returns the angle in the correct quadrant.  This assumes gravity lies in the x-y plane, the geometry AN-1057's own figure draws, with y reading about +1000 mg at rest.  Lab 7 asks you to check the axis directions marked on your own breakout board.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 4 min.  The lab is explicit: atan2, never plain atan.  One clause to land out loud: ax and ay are the milli-g numbers already on the screen, and the angle comes out the same from the raw values, because the scale factor cancels in the ratio.  Three axes orient you fully in space; the equations are in the Reference section and in AN-1057.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 9 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-float">
            <title>Part 9: Floating Point</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: connecting prose.  OWES, by id: a lead
                 paragraph recalling the integer conversion as the
                 before-picture (subsec-day13x-data-format by xref, one
                 beat, B-8), and a closing paragraph on cost (the no-FPU
                 sentence below stays; the flash-size tie to Lab 7 D4 is
                 written).  The cast question, its answer, and the
                 round()/%f delta are written now (P-18; the slides assert
                 them). -->
            <p>
                A sine or an arctangent does not come out of the integer
                arithmetic we have been writing; we need real numbers.
                C's type for them is
                <c>float</c> (single-precision floating point), 4 bytes,
                the same size as an <c>int32_t</c>, and the math functions
                come from <c>math.h</c>.  Before we convert the formula,
                recall <c>uart.c</c>'s baud-rate line
                (<xref ref="subsec-uart-brr"/>): there the division came
                out exact, and <c>uart.c</c> rounds rather than truncates,
                so nothing was lost.  Our conversion is not so lucky,
                because <c>/</c> between two integers throws the remainder
                away.  One more piece of C matters here: a cast applies
                only to the value written right after it, and it runs
                before <c>*</c> or <c>/</c>.  So <c>(float) 7 / 2</c>
                gives 3.5, while <c>(float)(7 / 2)</c> gives 3.0, because
                the division has already happened.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-day14-cast">
                <title>Place the cast</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-cast">
                    <statement><p>When dividing integers in C, the division
                    is performed in integer arithmetic: the remainder gets
                    lost, and the result is just the integer part.  The
                    conversion <c>ACCEL_raw * ACC_FS / 65536</c> divides that
                    way unless something is done about it.  Write the
                    expression with a
                    <c>(float)</c> cast placed so the result keeps its
                    fraction.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day14-cast-review">
                    <statement><p>As a review of how casting works in C: in
                    your expression, what exactly does the <c>(float)</c>
                    apply to, and when does it run relative to the
                    <c>*</c> and the <c>/</c>?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="ins-day14-cast">
                <p>One placement that works, with the cast on the raw
                value:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
float a = (float) ACCEL_raw * ACC_FS / 65536;    // 65536 = 2^16; the result is in g, not mg
]]></code></program>
                <p>It does not matter which operand carries the cast, as
                long as it happens before the division runs: <c>(float)
                ACCEL_raw * ACC_FS / 65536</c> and
                <c>ACCEL_raw * (float) ACC_FS / 65536</c> both keep the
                fraction.  As soon as one operand in the chain is a
                <c>float</c>, C computes the rest of that expression in
                floating point, left to right, so the multiply is a float
                multiply and the divide that follows is a float divide.
                What does not work is casting the finished integer result,
                <c>(float)(ACCEL_raw * ACC_FS / 65536)</c>: by then the
                division has already run in integer arithmetic (for us, to
                a whole number of g), and the fraction is already gone.
                Note the unit change: this form drops the
                <c>MILLI</c> factor, so <c>a</c> is in g rather than
                milli-g.  For sin⁻¹(A_x/g) that is the fraction of one g
                it needs, and for <c>atan2()</c> the scale cancels in the
                ratio anyway.</p>
            </instructor>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-format-recap">
    <p>Recall yesterday's integer conversion, which is what the float version is about to replace:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
a = (ACC_FS * ACCEL_raw * MILLI) >> ACC_REGISTERWIDTH;   // in mg
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The shift right by 16 divides by 2¹⁶ and floors the result: whole milli-g out, and the fraction is thrown away.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, one beat, the integer before-picture (Gate 1 R3): recall, not re-teach.  Her slide 19 re-showed the whole format page; one line is enough here.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-float">
    <ul>
        <li>A sine or an arctangent does not come out of integer arithmetic; we need real numbers.</li>
        <li>C's type for them is <c>float</c> (single-precision floating point): 4 bytes, the same size as an <c>int32_t</c>.</li>
        <li>The math functions come from <c>math.h</c>: <c>sin()</c>, <c>cos()</c>, <c>atan2()</c>, <c>round()</c>.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  Her slide 18's hinge ("Sine? Cosine? Tangent? Looks like we need floating point arithmetic") spoken, then this.  The cast question comes next: do not preview the answer.</note>
</slide>

            <!-- check-rules: allow L-2  (the slide recalls Day 5's no-%f
                 rule and teaches round() as the delta) -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-round">
    <ul>
        <li>Compute in float, but come back to integers to print: <c>printf</c>'s <c>%f</c> is not enabled in our projects, so the route is still convert, then <c>%d</c>.</li>
        <li>The new piece is <c>round()</c>, from <c>math.h</c>: where Day 5's cast to <c>int</c> truncated toward zero, <c>round()</c> goes to the nearest integer value, which you then cast for printing: <c>(int)round(angle)</c>.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min.  A delta on Day 5's rule, not a re-teach: the %f caveat is taught in ch-uart, and the enabling flag is shown in ch-debugging (Day 7x) via Lab 4 Appendix A.  If a student asks why flash grows: sin/cos/atan2/round are double-precision; the f-suffixed variants (atan2f, roundf) stay in float.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-cost">
    <ul>
        <li>The 32 bits of a <c>float</c> consist of a sign bit, an 8-bit exponent, and a 23-bit fraction: exactness is traded for range.</li>
        <li>The STM32C031C6 has no FPU (floating point unit), meaning no dedicated hardware circuit for float math, so every float operation is done in software.</li>
        <li>Software floats are slower than integer arithmetic, and the library code takes up flash memory.  In Lab 7 you are asked to measure exactly that: your program's size with the math library in it.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min.  Her slide 20's cost paragraph.  Depth (IEEE 754 layout, precision versus range) is in the chapter's Reference section, not here.  Her reassurance if precision comes up: our readings carry at most 12 bits, so a float's 24 lose nothing.</note>
</slide>

            <!-- check-rules: allow L-2  (this paragraph teaches the delta on
                 Day 5's no-%f rule: round() in place of the truncating
                 cast) -->
            <p>
                Compute in float, but come back to integers to print.
                Recall from Day 5 that <c>printf</c>'s <c>%f</c> is not
                enabled in our projects
                (<xref ref="subsec-uart-printf-usage"/>); the route is
                still convert-then-<c>%d</c>.  The new piece is
                <c>round()</c>, from <c>math.h</c>: where Day 5's cast to
                <c>int</c> truncated toward zero, <c>round()</c> goes to
                the nearest integer value, which you then cast for
                printing: <c>(int)round(angle)</c>.  That is what you want
                for an angle headed to a display.
            </p>

            <p>
                What does floating point cost?  A <c>float</c> spends its
                32 bits as a sign bit, an 8-bit exponent, and a 23-bit
                fraction, so it trades away exactness for range;
                <xref ref="sec-accel-reference"/> lays the format out.  The
                bigger cost is that the STM32C031C6 has no FPU (floating
                point unit), meaning no dedicated hardware circuit for
                float math, so every float operation is done in software:
                slower than integer arithmetic, and the library code takes
                up flash memory.  In Lab 7 you are asked to measure exactly that,
                your program's size with the math library in it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 10 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day14-lab7">
            <title>Part 10: Lab 7</title>

            <!-- DELIVERY 2: prose polish only.  The hand-off list is the
                 content and it is written; do not grow it. -->
            <p>
                Today's work is the preparation for Lab 7.  In the lab you
                are asked to run two I2C devices on the same bus at once,
                the accelerometer and the seven-segment display.  You are
                asked to compute and display tilt from
                −180° to 180°, with <c>atan2()</c> and <c>round()</c>.
                And you are asked to light orientation LEDs the way a phone
                does when its screen rotates; how you show the orientation
                is your own design decision.  The
                pre-lab is a working accelerometer driver, which is what
                you just built.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day14-lab7">
    <ul>
        <li>In Lab 7 you are asked to run two I2C devices on the same bus at once, the accelerometer and the seven-segment display.</li>
        <li>You are asked to compute and display tilt from −180° to 180°, with <c>atan2()</c> and <c>round()</c>.</li>
        <li>You are asked to light orientation LEDs the way a phone does when its screen rotates.  How you show the orientation is your own design decision.</li>
        <li>The pre-lab is a working accelerometer driver, which is what you just built.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 5 min, her slide 21.  Homework equals the pre-lab, already done by everyone who streamed data today.  The remaining minutes start Lab 7's shared-bus wiring with help in the room.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================
         Reference section WRITTEN 2026-08-31 (week 7 session 4), per
         the manifest in plans/day14-prompt.md: every OWES item from the
         earlier marker landed (C1/C2, register map/lsm303agr.h, data
         format in full, signed shift, zero-g calibration, AN-1057
         orientation, IEEE 754 precision-vs-range).  Code size is
         deliberately NOT here: Day 14 Part 9 teaches it and Lab 7 D4
         grades it.  Reference sections generate no slides.
         ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-accel-reference">
        <title>Reference: The LSM303AGR and Floating Point</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This section is lookup material for the accelerometer
                chapter.  Nothing here
                is new; it is the same information arranged in a fashion more
                suitable for a quick look-up, plus some additional depth in
                case you are curious.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accel-ref-mems">
            <title>The Differential Capacitors Inside the Sensor</title>
            <p>
                A MEMS (Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems) accelerometer
                measures its proof mass's displacement capacitively, and the
                real structure uses <em>two</em> capacitors per sense
                direction, not one.  The moving plate, attached to the proof
                mass, hangs between two fixed plates: it forms a capacitor
                C1 with one and a capacitor C2 with the other.  At rest the
                two gaps are equal, so C1 = C2.  When the case accelerates
                and the mass gets left behind, one gap narrows and the other
                widens, so C1 grows as C2 shrinks.
            </p>
            <p>
                The electronics measure the difference, ΔC = C1 − C2, which
                is zero at rest, grows with displacement, and carries the
                direction in its sign.  With a known excitation voltage
                V_exc across the plates, Q = CV turns the
                capacitance difference into a charge difference,
                <m>\Delta Q = (C_1 - C_2)\,V_{exc}</m>, which the chip's
                amplifier converts to a voltage for the ADC
                (analog-to-digital converter).  Measuring the difference of a matched pair,
                rather than one capacitance alone, cancels effects that move
                both capacitors together, such as temperature.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accel-ref-registers">
            <title>The Register Map, and lsm303agr.h</title>
            <p>
                The LSM303AGR's register address map is §7, Table 26
                (p. 43) of the
                <url href="external/datasheets/lsm303agr.pdf">datasheet</url>;
                <c>lsm303agr.h</c> is the part of that table this driver
                uses, transcribed into
                <c>#define</c>s, plus the driver's data structure and
                prototypes.  Here is the header:
            </p>

            <!-- Displayed copy of assets/starters/lsm303agr.h.  Matches the
                 file in CODE; not registered in check_starters.py because
                 the display copy normalizes the file's internal tab
                 whitespace so the DEFAULT/TYPE columns line up (Petra,
                 2026-08-30, same ruling as the Day 13 excerpt), and two
                 stale-revision citations in comments are corrected to the
                 hosted PDF (the file says "Sec 6, 7.1" and "Section 7.1";
                 the hosted map is §7, Table 26), and the file's
                 "Borrows from Adafruit_LSM303_U.h and my MS8607.h"
                 attribution line is elided (internal provenance, B-11e).
                 Change file and listing together. -->
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Simple C library for the Adafruit LSM303AGR accelerometer/magnetometer.
// ENGS 28
// Accelerometer only.

// Sensor calibration constants (Datasheet, Section 2)
#define ACCEL_GRAVITY       9.8         // "one g"
#define ACCEL_RANGE         2           // g, see CTRL_REG4

// I2C address for the sensor
#define LSM303_ADDRESS_ACCEL    (0x32 >> 1)         // 0011001x

// ACCELEROMETER SECTION
// Accelerometer registers (Datasheet 7, Table 26)  // DEFAULT    TYPE
#define LSM303_WHO_AM_I_A          0x0F   // 00110011   r
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG1_A         0x20   // 00000111   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG2_A         0x21   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG3_A         0x22   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG4_A         0x23   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG5_A         0x24   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_CTRL_REG6_A         0x25   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_REFERENCE_A         0x26   // 00000000   rw
#define LSM303_STATUS_REG_A        0x27   // 00000000   r
#define LSM303_OUT_X_L_A           0x28
#define LSM303_OUT_X_H_A           0x29
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_L_A           0x2A
#define LSM303_OUT_Y_H_A           0x2B
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_L_A           0x2C
#define LSM303_OUT_Z_H_A           0x2D
#define LSM303_FIFO_CTRL_REG_A     0x2E
#define LSM303_FIFO_SRC_REG_A      0x2F
#define LSM303_INT1_CFG_A          0x30
#define LSM303_INT1_SRC_A          0x31
#define LSM303_INT1_THS_A          0x32
#define LSM303_INT1_DURATION_A     0x33
#define LSM303_INT2_CFG_A          0x34
#define LSM303_INT2_SRC_A          0x35
#define LSM303_INT2_THS_A          0x36
#define LSM303_INT2_DURATION_A     0x37
#define LSM303_CLICK_CFG_A         0x38
#define LSM303_CLICK_SRC_A         0x39
#define LSM303_CLICK_THS_A         0x3A
#define LSM303_TIME_LIMIT_A        0x3B
#define LSM303_TIME_LATENCY_A      0x3C
#define LSM303_TIME_WINDOW_A       0x3D

// Because each sensor outputs an (x, y, z) triple for acceleration field and
// magnetic field, it makes sense to put these in structures.
typedef struct{
        int16_t x;
        int16_t y;
        int16_t z;
    } lsm303AccelData_s;

// C function prototypes for accelerometer driver
// These are low-level functions that interact with the registers via I2C,
// and are called by higher-level functions used by the top
// level application.

// The accelerometer has several gain and update rate settings.  These are described
// in the datasheet and are set up in the AccelInit function.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelInit();

// The AccelRegisterWrite function should take a register address and a command byte
// to be written into the register.
void lsm303_AccelRegisterWrite(uint8_t RegisterAddress, uint8_t data);

// The AccelRegisterRead function should take a register address and return a data byte
// read from the register.
uint8_t lsm303_AccelRegisterRead(uint8_t RegisterAddress);

// Here is the higher-level function to read the acceleration vector.  Output struct
// contains raw (unscaled) acceleration values.
void lsm303_AccelReadRaw(lsm303AccelData_s * const result);
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The <c>_A</c> suffix marks the accelerometer's half of the
                register map; the magnetometer has its own registers, which
                this course does not use.  The registers from 0x2E on
                (FIFO, meaning first-in-first-out buffering, plus
                interrupts and click detection) stay at their defaults.
                The comment columns record the control and status
                registers' power-on defaults and access types, from
                Table 26.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accel-ref-data-format">
            <title>The Data Format, and the Conversion in All Three Modes</title>
            <p>
                Each axis's reading is 16 bits (two data registers, low byte
                at the lower address), left-justified, two's complement.
                The three resolution modes write 8, 10, or 12 valid bits
                into the top of the same 16-bit word, zeros below.  Each
                mode's conversion is: shift the zero bits off the bottom,
                then multiply by that mode's step size at the chosen
                full-scale range.  At the ±2 g range (4 g of span) the
                three modes are as follows.
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths="86%" margins="7%">
            <tabular halign="left">
                <col width="34%"/><col width="16%"/><col width="16%"/><col width="34%"/>
                <row header="yes"><cell>Mode</cell><cell>Valid bits</cell><cell>Shift</cell><cell>Step size at ±2 g</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Low-power</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>≫ 8</cell><cell>4 g / 2⁸ = 15.625 mg</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Normal</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>≫ 6</cell><cell>4 g / 2¹⁰ = 3.90625 mg</cell></row>
                <row><cell>High-resolution</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>≫ 4</cell><cell>4 g / 2¹² = 0.9765625 mg</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            </sidebyside>
            <p>
                All three collapse to one formula, because the valid bits
                always start at bit 15: the shift and the step size cancel
                to 2¹⁶, giving
                <m>a = \mathrm{FS} \cdot \mathrm{raw} / 2^{16}</m> with FS
                the full-scale span (4 g here) and the raw value read as a
                signed 16-bit integer.  Change the range and the constant
                changes with it: at ±4 g the span is 8 g, and so on.
            </p>
            <p>
                Here is a worked negative example, in low-power mode at
                ±2 g: the
                reading 0xC000 has bit 15 set, so it is negative.  By the
                top byte: 0xC0 is −128 + 64 = −64, and
                −64 × 15.625 mg = −1000 mg.  By the whole word:
                0xC000 as a signed 16-bit integer is −32768 + 16384 =
                −16384, and 4 g × (−16384) / 2¹⁶ = −1000 mg.  Both routes
                always agree; the collapsed formula is just the top-byte
                route with the shift folded into the constant.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accel-ref-signed-shift">
            <title>Shifting a Signed Value Is Not Quite Dividing It</title>
            <p>
                <c>accel_test.c</c> converts with
                <c>(ACC_FS * raw * MILLI) &gt;&gt; ACC_REGISTERWIDTH</c>.
                The variables are <c>int16_t</c>, and the large
                intermediate product (up to about ±131 million) exists only
                inside the arithmetic, which C carries out in a wider
                integer type before the shift brings the result back within
                ±2000.  On a signed value, our compiler's <c>&gt;&gt;</c>
                is an
                <term>arithmetic shift</term>: it shifts the sign bit back
                in at the top, and it rounds toward negative infinity (it is
                a <em>floor</em>).  The C standard leaves that choice to
                the implementation, and arm-none-eabi-gcc, like every
                compiler you are likely to meet, shifts the sign bit back
                in.  Integer division with <c>/</c> rounds
                toward zero instead.  For positive values the two agree; for
                negative values they can differ by one.
            </p>
            <p>
                That difference is visible in this chapter's own numbers,
                because the program multiplies by 4000 <em>before</em>
                shifting by 16, and the product is usually not a multiple of
                2¹⁶.  Take a raw reading of −64 (0xFFC0), which is one step
                in normal mode: the product is
                4 × (−64) × 1000 = −256000, which is −3.90625 of 2¹⁶, the
                chapter's own step size.  The
                shift floors it to −4, so CoolTerm prints −4 mg; a hand
                check with <c>/</c> or a calculator truncates to −3 mg.  So
                a hand-converted negative reading can land 1 mg away from
                what the program prints, and neither is wrong; they round
                differently.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accel-ref-offset">
            <title>Calibrating the Zero-g Offset</title>
            <p>
                The datasheet's sensor-characteristics table (§2.1, Table 3)
                says a flat, motionless sensor typically reads ±40 mg away
                from the true value on an axis, and its Min and Max columns
                allow ±80 mg.  The offset is stable for a given board at a
                given temperature, so
                you can measure it and subtract it.  Read the z axis flat
                (expect +1000 mg) and flipped (expect −1000 mg); the offset
                is the average of the two readings, because the true ±1000
                cancels in the sum.  If a board averages +1032 mg flat and
                −968 mg flipped, the offset is (1032 + (−968)) / 2 =
                +32 mg, and future readings are corrected by subtracting
                32 mg.  This is the two-point calibration of AN-1057
                (its Equation 17: the offset is half the sum of the +1 g
                and −1 g measurements), applied to the z axis.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accel-ref-orientation">
            <title>Full Orientation from Three Axes (AN-1057)</title>
            <p>
                The two-axis arctangent gives one tilt angle.
                <url href="external/datasheets/an-1057.pdf">AN-1057</url>
                (p. 7, Equations 11–13) extends this to a full orientation:
                measure each axis's angle against the horizon using the
                other two axes as the reference,
                <md>
                    \theta = \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{A_x}{\sqrt{A_y^2 + A_z^2}}\right),\qquad
                    \psi = \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{A_y}{\sqrt{A_x^2 + A_z^2}}\right),\qquad
                    \varphi = \tan^{-1}\!\left(\frac{\sqrt{A_x^2 + A_y^2}}{A_z}\right)
                </md>
                where θ and ψ are the x and y axes' angles above the
                horizon and φ is the angle between the gravity vector and
                the z axis.  Because each is an arctangent of a ratio of
                accelerations, the two-axis benefits carry over: constant
                sensitivity, and validity at every orientation of the
                sensor.  This is the route for Lab 7's optional
                Going Further.
            </p>
            <p>
                On quadrants: a plain arctangent only returns angles in a
                half circle, because it cannot tell
                <m>(+A_x, +A_y)</m> from <m>(-A_x, -A_y)</m>: the ratio is
                the same.  <c>atan2(ax, ay)</c> takes the two values
                separately, looks at both signs to pick the correct
                quadrant, and returns an angle over the full circle, from
                −π to π (in radians; multiply by 180/π for degrees).  It
                also handles <c>ay</c> = 0, where the plain ratio would
                divide by zero.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accel-ref-float">
            <title>The float Format: Precision Traded for Range</title>
            <p>
                The 32 bits of a <c>float</c> (single-precision floating
                point, IEEE 754)
                consist of 1 sign bit, an 8-bit exponent, and a
                23-bit fraction, and represent
                <m>N = \pm\,\mathrm{fraction} \times 2^{\mathrm{exponent}}</m>:
                like scientific notation, but in base 2.  The stored 23 fraction bits plus
                an implied leading 1 give about 24 significant bits, which
                is roughly 7 decimal digits of relative precision, while
                the exponent lets the value range over about 10⁻³⁸ to
                10³⁸.
            </p>
            <p>
                Compare an <c>int32_t</c>: it is <em>exact</em> at every
                one of its values over its full −2³¹ to 2³¹−1 range, but it
                has no
                fractions and nothing beyond that range.  A <c>float</c> of
                the same 32 bits carries only ~24 significant bits of
                precision, but places them anywhere in an enormous range.
                Precision and range are traded against the same 32 bits;
                neither type gets both.  For our sensor the trade is easy:
                the readings carry at most 12 significant bits, so a
                float's 24 lose nothing, and the trigonometry needs
                fractions.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
